= Simulation framework
<sec:simnexp_details>
A n-SiC/SiO$""_2$/n$""^(+)$-Si MOSC was used for both the simulation and experimental measurement of the gate leakage current under a sweep of gate bias (1 V/s) at temperatures from 80 to 573 K. This device was fabricated according to the process detailed in Ref. @moensChargetoBreakdownQBDApproach2020a and has a donor concentration of $N_(upright("d")) = 1.0 times 10^16 upright(" cm")^(- 3)$, a polycrystalline n$""^(+)$-Si gate, and a 54-nm thermally-grown a-SiO$""_2$ which was exposed to a NO post oxidation annealling.

The detailed physical models required to capture all described effects have been implemented in the open-source simulator Comphy @Comphy_Rzepa2018@Comphyv3_Waldoer2023. For simplicity, the elementary charge has been set to unity in this work.

== Gate leakage current in MOSC
<gate-leakage-current-in-mosc>
#figure(mos-capacitor,
  caption: [
    The 1D MOSC model for gate leakage current simulation. Top: External circuit used for analysis, with voltage and conventional current (arrow) defined as positive. Middle: Illustrative space-charge profile $rho \( z \)$ showing oxide charge trapping and possible interface charges at the SiC/a‑SiO$""_2$ and n$""^(+)$-Si/a‑SiO$""_2$ interfaces, respectively. Bottom: Illustrative current density profile $J \( z \)$ across the MOSC, indicating Fowler-Nordheim tunneling near $z_(upright("tm"))$ and amplification of electron and hole currents via impact ionization.
  ]
)
<fig_1dmoscap>

@fig_1dmoscap depicts the one-dimensional MOSC (1D MOSC) model used to analyze the gate leakage current. An a-SiO$""_2$ layer of thickness $L$ separates an n-type 4H-SiC from an n$""^(+)$-Si gate, each contacted by a metal electrode. A time-dependent gate bias $V_(upright("g")) \( t \)$ is applied to gate electrode, and the gate leakage current $J_(upright("g")) \( t \)$ is measured in the external circuit. Upon assuming translational invariance along the lateral directions, the electrostatic potential $phi \( z \, t \)$ obeys the 1D Poisson equation $ frac(partial, partial z) [epsilon.alt \( z \) frac(partial phi \( z \, t \), partial z)] = - rho \( z \, t \) $<eqn_Poissoneq_general> where $epsilon.alt \( z \)$ is the local permittivity and $rho \( z \, t \)$ comprises free-carrier and fixed charges. Solving Eq. @eqn_Poissoneq_general in the SiC and n$""^(+)$-Si regions yields the surface potential $phi_(upright("s")) \( t \)$, $phi_(upright("g")) \( t \)$ and their corresponding surface charges $Q_(upright("s")) \( t \)$, $Q_(upright("g")) \( t \)$, including any interface-trapped charges $Q_(upright("it,s")) \( t \)$, $Q_(upright("it,g")) \( t \)$. Enforcing the Kirchhoff's voltage law @tsividisOperationModelingMOS2011 across the stack gives $ phi_(upright("s")) \( t \) - frac(Q_(upright("s")) \( t \) + Q_(upright("it,s")) \( t \), C_(upright("ox"))) + Delta V_(upright("ox,s")) \( L \, t \) & = V_(upright("g")) \( t \) - phi.alt_(upright("ms")) + phi_(upright("g")) \( t \)\
phi_(upright("s")) \( t \) + frac(Q_(upright("g")) \( t \) + Q_(upright("it,g")) \( t \), C_(upright("ox"))) + Delta V_(upright("ox,g")) \( 0 \, t \) & = V_(upright("g")) \( t \) - phi.alt_(upright("ms")) + phi_(upright("g")) \( t \) $<eqns_surfpot> where $C_(upright("ox")) = epsilon.alt_(upright("ox")) \/ L$, $phi.alt_(upright("ms"))$ the metal-semiconductor work-function difference, and $Delta V_(upright("ox,s")) \( L \, t \)$ \[$Delta V_(upright("ox,g")) \( 0 \, t \)$\] the voltage drop across the oxide due to trapped charges that is projected to the SiC/a-SiO$""_2$ (n$""^(+)$-Si/a-SiO$""_2$) interface. When charge trapping in bulk oxide is significant, the potential profile must be solved self-consistently with the trap-occupancy dynamics.

According to the continuity equation, the gate leakage current $J_(upright("g")) \( t \)$ can be expressed as $ J_(upright("g")) \( t \) = J_n \( L^(upright("-")) \) + frac(partial \( Q_(upright("g")) + Q_(upright("it,g")) \), partial V_(upright("g"))) frac(upright(d) V_(upright("g")), upright(d) t) $<eqn_Jgt_ful> in which the first term is the #emph[net] electron current arriving at the n$""^(+)$-Si/a-SiO$""_2$ interface, and the second term is the displacement current with $partial \( Q_(upright("g")) + Q_(upright("it,g")) \) \/ partial V_(upright("g"))$ representing the incremental capacitance of the gate side . In typical quasi-static measurement, the displacement current can be ignored due to the long-enough integration time. In this work, both charg capture (emission) and trap creation at the interface have been neglected. Hence, one obtains $ J_(upright("g")) \( t \) approx J_(upright("n")) \( L^(upright("-")) \) . $<eqn_Jg_JnL> where $J_(upright("n")) \( L^(upright("-")) \)$ is the electron current arriving at the n$""^(+)$-Si/a-SiO$""_2$ interface.

Under forward bias, the $J_(upright("g")) \( t \)$ is entirely determined by $J_(upright("n")) \( L^(upright("-")) \)$ because hole current $J_(upright("p")) \( L^(upright("-")) \)$ is negligible. Physically, $J_(upright("n")) \( L^(upright("-")) \)$ arises from (i) quantum injection of electrons from the conduction band of SiC conduction band into that of a-SiO$""_2$ and (ii) field-driven transport and amplification via impact-ionization in a-SiO$""_2$. A brute-force quantum mechanical solution across tens of nanometers of oxide is computationally prohibitive, especially when charge trapping must also be included in the oxide at every disctetized time step. Therefore, we use a “tunneling + transport\" approach @tunnelingptransp_FilipJVSTB1999@Fischetti_shortcutpaperJAP1995, which is supported by experimental transport studies @ballistictransportaSiO2_DiMariaPRL1986@BallisticTrans_FischettiPhysRevB1987. First, the energy distribution function (EDF) of electrons that just tunnel into the CB of a-SiO$""_2$ will be calculated by a simplified tunneling model (see sec. @sec:tunneling). Second, the evolution of EDFs and amplification of the electron current is obtained by solving a simplified 1D Boltzmann transport equation (BTE) where impact ionization is also incorporated (see Sec. @sec:transport). The trapped charges in a-SiO$""_2$ influence the tunneling and transport by altering the potential profile $phi \( z \)$. Within this framework, the electron current and the electron concentration are, respectively, $ J_(upright("n")) \( z \, t \) & = n_(upright("ox")) \( z \, t \) v_(upright("d")) \( z \, t \)\
n_(upright("ox")) \( z \, t \) & = n_(upright("FN")) \( z_(upright("t,m")) \) times exp (integral_(z_(upright("t,m")))^z gamma_(upright("ox")) \( x \) upright(d) x) $<eqn_elcurrent> in which $v_(upright("d")) \( z \)$ is the average drift velocity, $gamma_(upright("ox")) \( z \)$ the electron-initiated impact ionization coefficient in a-SiO$""_2$ and $n_(upright("FN")) \( z_(upright("tm")) \)$ the concentration of electrons that is injected into the CB of a-SiO$""_2$ by Fowler-Norheim tunneling at the maximum classical turning point $z_(upright("tm"))$. The hole initiated impact ionization in a-SiO$""_2$ has been neglected because of the high relaxation rate of (hot) holes @holeIIquartz_KunikiyoJAP941096 and the low hole mobility in a-SiO$""_2$ @holemobiaSiO2_HughesAPL264362. Hence, the hole current and hole concentration can be obtained as $ J_(upright("p")) \( z \, t \) & = J_(upright("n")) \( L^(upright("-")) \) [1 - exp (- integral_z^L gamma_(upright("ox")) \( x \) upright(d) x)]\
p_(upright("ox")) \( z \, t \) & = J_(upright("p")) \( z \, t \) \/ v_(upright("h")) \( z \, t \) $<eqn_holecurrent> where $v_(upright("h")) \( z \, t \)$ is the average drift velocity of holes. The subsequent capture of those holes in bulk a-SiO$""_2$ reshapes the electrostatic potential and hence indirectly contributes to the gate leakage current.

== Electrostatics
<sec:electrostatics>
The potential profile $phi \( z \)$ of the 1D MOSC spans three regions, i.e., the SiC substrate, the oxide a-SiO$""_2$, and the n$""^(+)$-Si gate. The n$""^(+)$-Si gate is treated as a heavily doped semiconductor rather than a metal. In the SiC and n$""^(+)$-Si, the space charge space-charge density arises from both free carriers and ionized dopants. Within the oxide, mobile carriers are negligible and the charge density is dominated by trapped charges.

=== Electrostatics of SiC and n$""^(+)$-Si
<electrostatics-of-sic-and-n-si>
Upon assuming thermal equilibrium, the electrostatic potential in each semiconductor region satisfies $ epsilon.alt_s frac(upright(d)^2 phi \( z \), upright(d) z^2) = - [p \( phi \) - n \( phi \) + N_(upright("d"))^(+) \( phi \) - N_(upright("a"))^(-) \( phi \)] $<Poissoneq> where $n \( phi \)$ and $p \( phi \)$ denote the electron and hole concentrations, $N_(upright("d"))^(+) \( phi \)$ and $N_(upright("a"))^(-) \( phi \)$ are the concentrations of ionized donors and acceptors, respectively. It is noteworthy that charged point defects in semiconductors can be modeled as donors or acceptors according to their levels in the band gap. Solving this equation requires models for both carriers statistics and dopant ionization as a function of $phi$.

Unpon assuming thermal equilibrium, the free-carrier concentrations follow directly from Fermi-Dirac (FD) statistics applied to the band-structure and the corresponding density of states (DOS). Denoting by $E_parallel$ and $E_perp$ the kinetic energies associated with motion parallel to $z$ ($k_parallel$) and within the $k_x - k_y$ plane ($k_perp$), we obtain $ n \( phi \) & = sum_i integral.double_0^oo frac(g_(upright("c") \, i) \( E_parallel \, E_perp \) upright(d) E_parallel upright(d) E_perp, 1 + exp \[ beta \( E_parallel + E_perp + E_(upright("CF") \, i) - phi \) \]) \,\
p \( phi \) & = sum_j integral.double_0^oo frac(g_(upright("v") \, j) \( E_parallel \, E_perp \) upright(d) E_parallel upright(d) E_perp, 1 + exp \[ beta \( E_parallel + E_perp - E_(upright("FV") \, j) + phi \) \]) \, $<eqn_elhconc> where $beta = 1 \/ k_(upright("B")) T \, E_(upright("CF") \, i) = E_(upright("C") \, i) - E_(upright("F"))$, and $E_(upright("FV") \, j) = E_(upright("F")) - E_(upright("V") \, j)$. The index $i$ ($j$) runs over conduction (valence) subbands, each with DOS function $g_(upright("c") \, i)$ ($g_(upright("v") \, j)$). In 4H-SiC with $k_parallel = \[ 001 \]$ and $k_perp$ in the $Gamma$MK plane, the conduction band comprises two Kane-type nonparabolic subbands separated by a 0.115 eV mini-gap, while the valence band is represented by three parabolic subbands, as illustrated in @fig_SiCbandstruct. Details of the band models for a-SiO$""_2$ and n$""^(+)$-Si are given in Appendix @sec:App_bandstructure.

#figure(image("source/SiCCBVBstructure.png", width: 100%),
