  ]
)
<fig_SiCbandstruct>

Each dopant species at crystallographic site $i$ (donor) or $j$ (acceptor) ionizes according to the local potential $phi$ and the site's ionization energy $E_(upright("d") \, i)$ or $E_(upright("a") \, j)$. The thermal-equilibrium ionization fractions are $ frac(N_(upright("d") \, i)^(+) \( phi \), N_(upright("d") \, i)) & = 1 - frac(b \( N_(upright("d")) \), 1 + g_(upright("d"))^(- 1) exp {- beta [E_(upright("d") \, i) \( N_(upright("d")) \) - E_(upright("CF")) + phi]}) \,\
frac(N_(upright("a") \, j)^(-) \( phi \), N_(upright("a") \, j)) & = 1 - frac(b \( N_(upright("a")) \), 1 + g_(upright("a"))^(- 1) exp {- beta [E_(upright("a") \, j) \( N_(upright("a")) \) - E_(upright("FV")) - phi]}) \, $<eqn_ionized_dopants> where $g_(upright("d"))$ ($g_(upright("a"))$) is the donor (acceptor) degeneracy factor, and $b \( N \)$ accounts for dopant clustering effects. Here the ionization energy $E_(upright("d") \, i) \( N_(upright("d")) \)$ and $E_(upright("a") \, j) \( N_(upright("a")) \)$ may shift with local concentration via band-gap narrowing or Coulomb interactions. The Fermi level offset $E_(upright("CF"))$ ($E_(upright("FV"))$) is defined as the separation of the Fermi level $E_(upright("F"))$ from the CB minimum $E_(upright("C"))$ (VB maximum $E_(upright("V"))$) $ E_(upright("CF")) \( T \) = E_(upright("C")) \( T \) - E_(upright("F")) \( T \) \, E_(upright("FV")) \( T \) = E_(upright("F")) \( T \) - E_(upright("V")) \( T \) . $<eqn_Fermilevel_offset> Both the offsets and the ionized-dopant concentrations are obtained by enforcing global charge neutrality, so their temperature dependence follows directly from the band-structure and ionization models. Detailed parameterizations of $E_(upright("d") \, i)$ or $E_(upright("a") \, j)$ and $b \( N \)$ are given in the Appendix @sec:APP_incompleteionization.

=== Electrostatics in a-SiO$""_2$
<electrostatics-in-a-sio_2>
The space charge in the oxide $ rho_(upright("ox")) \( z \, t \) = n_(upright("ox")) \( z \, t \) + p_(upright("ox")) \( z \, t \) + rho_(upright("ox,t")) \( z \, t \) . $ comprises electrons in the CB $n_(upright("ox")) \( z \)$, holes in the VB $p_(upright("ox")) \( z \)$, and charges trapped in the oxide $n_(upright("ox,t")) \( z \)$. The charges in the intervals $\( 0 \, z \)$ and $\( z \, L \)$ lead to potential shifts relative to the SiC/a-SiO$""_2$ and the n$""^(+)$Si /a-SiO$""_2$ interfaces, respectively, $ Delta V_(upright("ox,s")) \( z \, t \) & = 1 / epsilon.alt_(upright("ox")) integral_0^z \( z - x \) rho_(upright("ox")) \( x \, t \) upright(d) x \,\
Delta V_(upright("ox,g")) \( z \, t \) & = 1 / epsilon.alt_(upright("ox")) integral_L^z \( z - x \) rho_(upright("ox")) \( x \, t \) upright(d) x $<eqn_dVox> which enter Eq. @eqns_surfpot. We update $rho_(upright("ox")) \( z \, t \)$ in successive quasi-static time steps: $n_(upright("ox")) \( z \, t \)$ and $p_(upright("ox")) \( z \, t \)$ directly follow from the transport solution (see Sec. @sec:transport), while $rho_(upright("ox,t")) \( z \, t \)$ is governed by the trapping dynamics.

The concentration of trapped oxide charges is obtained by integrating the product of the local DOS $g_(upright("t")) \( E_(upright("t")) \, z \, upright("P") \)$ and the corresponding occupation function $f \( E_(upright("t")) \, z \, upright("P") \, t \)$ over trap energy $E_(upright("t"))$ and other trap parameter set $upright("P")$ $ rho_(upright("ox,t")) \( z \, t \) = integral.double g_(upright("t")) \( E_(upright("t")) \, z \, upright("P") \) f \( E_(upright("t")) \, z \, upright("P") \, t \) upright(d) E_(upright("t")) upright(d) upright("P") $ Often, $z$ and $upright("P")$ will be omitted for clarity in the following. Each trap is modeled as a two-state system: state 1 corresponds to an electron-filled defect, and state 2 to an empty defect. The electron occupation $f \( E_t \, t \)$ function satisfies $ frac(partial f \( E_(upright("t")) \, t \), partial t) = - k_12 \( E_(upright("t")) \) f \( E_(upright("t")) \, t \) + \[ 1 - f \( E_(upright("t")) \, t \) \] k_21 \( E_(upright("t")) \) $ where $k_21 \( E_(upright("t")) \)$ and $k_12 \( E_(upright("t")) \)$ are the capture and emission rate, respectively, and are dependent on temperature and the local potential $phi \( z \)$ @grasserStochasticChargeTrapping2012. As illustrated in @fig_chargecapture, a charge in a trap in the oxide can exchange charge with three types of reserviors: (i) the CB and/or VB of a semiconductor/metal across the interface with the oxide @shinModelingCarrierTrapping2025 (@fig_chargecapture#text[a]), (ii) the CB and/or VB of the oxide itself (@fig_chargecapture#text[b]) @multipletrapping_Curtis, and (iii) another trap in the oxide @TATpartII_Schleich2022@schleichSingleMultiStepTrap2022, which is not considered in the present study.

All these charge-exchange processes are mediated by nonradiative multiphonon (NMP) processes @grasserStochasticChargeTrapping2012@grasserBiasTemperatureInstability2014. The potential energy surfaces (PES) for the two charge states of a defect are often modeled as 1D parabolas as a function of a generalized reaction coordinate $q$, as shown in @fig_chargecapture#text[a]. For defects in a-SiO$""_2$, the curvature ratio is assumed to be unity, consitent with linear electron-phonon coupling @goesIdentificationOxideDefects2018. In the the high-temperature-strong-coupling limit, the state-to-state transition probabilities are, respectively, $ k_(12 \, 0) \( E_(upright("t")) \) & = frac(c_0 D \( E_(upright("t")) \, z_t \), sqrt(beta E_(upright("R")))) exp (- beta epsilon_12) \,\
k_(21 \, 0) \( E_(upright("t")) \) & = frac(c_0 D \( E_(upright("t")) \, z_t \), sqrt(beta E_(upright("R")))) exp (- beta epsilon_21) $ in which $D \( E_(upright("t")) \)$ is the tunneling factor, $E_(upright("R"))$ the relaxation energy, and $c_0$ an energy and temperature independent constant @huangTheoryLightAbsorption1950@kuboApplicationMethodGenerating1955@goesIdentificationOxideDefects2018. In addition, $epsilon_12$ and $epsilon_12$ are the activation energies for the $1 arrow.r 2$ and $2 arrow.r 1$ transitions, respectively, and for linear electron-phonon coupling they are $ epsilon_12 = frac((E_21 + E_(upright("R")))^2, 4 E_(upright("R"))) \, epsilon_21 = epsilon_12 - E_21 $<eqn_barrierNMP> where $E_21 = V_2 \( q_2 \) - V_1 \( q_1 \)$ is the PES energy difference @grasserStochasticChargeTrapping2012. When a defect interacts with charge reservoirs across the interface, the total rates can be obtained according to the band-edge approximation @Comphy_Rzepa2018. For example, the emission and capture rates for interaction with the CB of a semiconductor are respectively $ k_12 \( E_t \) & = exp [beta (E_(upright("CF")) - phi_(upright("s")))] n \( T \, phi_(upright("s")) \) v_(upright("th")) \( T \) sigma_(upright("s")) k_(12 \, 0) \( E_(upright("t")) \)\
k_21 \( E_(upright("t")) \) & = n \( T \, phi_(upright("s")) \) v_(upright("th")) \( T \) sigma_(upright("s")) k_(21 \, 0) \( E_(upright("t")) \) $ in which $n \( T \, phi_(upright("s")) \)$ is the electron concentration at the interface, $v_(upright("th")) \( T \)$ the thermal velocity of electrons and $sigma_(upright("s"))$ the effective cross-section @rzepaEfficientPhysicalModeling2018.

