<fig_chargecapture>

When a trap exchange with the CB and/or VB of the oxide, it can take place locally and non-locally as illustrated in @fig_chargecapture#text[b]. A trap can exchange charge locally with the CB and VB at the same spatial location $z$ for which the tunneling factor $D \( E_(upright("t")) \, z_(upright("t")) \)$ in $k_(12 \, 0)$ and $k_(21 \, 0)$ are both unity. Such a model is referred to as multiple trapping (MT) model, and has been able to explain the features of experimental hole mobility in a-SiO$""_2$ @multipletrapping_Curtis. The charge exchange can also occur via tunneling through a finite potential barrier via an NMP transition, a process often termed phonon-assisted tunneling @makram-ebeidQuantumModelPhononassisted1982. Considering the charge exchange with the VB of a-SiO$""_2$, we can write the total emission and capture rates as, $ k_12 \( E_(upright("t")) \) & = frac(c_0 sigma_(upright("ox,h")), sqrt(beta E_(upright("R")))) #scale(x: 180%, y: 180%)[\[] exp \( - beta epsilon_12 \) J_(upright("p")) \( z \)\
 &  + sum_(j = - n)^(j = n) J_(upright("p")) \( z_(upright("FN")) \) 3 / 2 Theta_j exp (- beta epsilon_(12 \, j) - 2 Theta_j) #scale(x: 180%, y: 180%)[\]]\
k_21 \( E_(upright("t")) \) & = frac(c_0 sigma_(upright("ox,h")) macron(N_(upright("p"))), sqrt(beta E_(upright("R")))) #scale(x: 180%, y: 180%)[\[] exp \( - beta epsilon_21 \)\
 &  + sum_(j = - n)^(j = n) 3 / 2 Theta_j exp (- beta epsilon_(21 \, j) - 2 Theta_j) #scale(x: 180%, y: 180%)[\]] $<eqn_k12k21MT> where the hole fluxes $J_(upright("p")) \( z \)$ and $J_(upright("p")) \( z_(upright("FN")) \)$ indicate the local and non-local transitions, respectively, and $macron(N_(upright("p")))$ characterizes an averaged flux of electrons in the VB. The barriers and phase-factor $Theta_j$ are as follows, $ epsilon_(12 \, j) & = (E_(upright("R")) - j ℏ omega)^2 \/ 4 E_(upright("R")) \, epsilon_(21 \, j) = epsilon_(12 \, j) + j ℏ omega \,\
Theta_j & = 1 / ℏ integral_(z_(upright("FN")))^(z_t) sqrt(2 m_(upright("ox") \, h) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\|] E_21 \( z \) + j ℏ omega #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\|]) upright(d) z \,\
E_21 \( z \) & = E_(upright("v")) \( z \) - E_t \( z \) .\
 $ Only one phonon-mode of single frequency $omega$ is considered for the phonon-assisted tunneling processes, which are manifested in $Theta_j exp \( - 2 Theta_j \)$ in Eq. @eqn_k12k21MT.

== Quantum Tunneling Models
<sec:tunneling>
We restrict our attention to electron tunneling from the SiC conduction band into the conduction band of the gate dielectric (a-SiO$""_2$), since this process dominates gate leakage in SiC MOSFETs; hole tunneling follows the same principles. In most conventional treatments, the tunneling current density is computed via Bardeen's transfer-Hamiltonian formalism @Tunneling_BardenPRL1961 combined with the Tsu--Esaki approach @Tunneling_TsuEsaki, and the transmission probability is taken from Fowler--Nordheim or Gundlach analytic expressions for triangular or trapezoidal barriers @FowlerNordheim1928@trapezoidbarrier_Gundlach, respectively. Two key approximations underlie these methods, yet both break down for reliability assessment of thick (tens of nanometers) oxides in power devices. First, semiconductor carriers in the inversion or accumulation layer are modeled as free-electron gases, neglecting the quantization of subbands that arises under strong surface fields. Second, the tunneling is assumed to be scattering-free such that inelastic processes, which are unavoidable for transport at high electric field, are ignored completely.

To overcome these limitations, we introduce a two-stage "tunneling + transport" framework. In the first stage, we map the confined inversion/accumulation layer states onto an equivalent energy-resolved distribution of free electrons incident on the oxide, and calculate their transmission into a-SiO$""_2$ using a modified transfer-Hamiltonian approach that includes image-force corrections and electric-field-induced band bending. In the second stage, the EDF of tunneled electrons is advanced through the oxide by a 1D BTE, which explicitly accounts for electron-phonon scattering and impact ionization (see Sec. @sec:transport).

=== Tunneling of electrons in the space-charge region
<tunneling-of-electrons-in-the-space-charge-region>
#figure(image("source/TunnelingModel_mapping.png", width: 100%),
  caption: [
    Methodology for modeling tunneling from the semiconductor space-charge region into the insulator. (a) Device electrostatics at $V_(upright("g")) = 40$ V, T = 298 K: carrier and ionized dopant profiles (top) under band bending (bottom), showing local carrier contributions $Delta Q_n \( z_k \)$ to the surface charge. (b) Implementation of a weighting scheme for tunneling: (i) weight function $W_q \( z \)$ derived from surface charge contributions, (ii) energy-resolved electron distributions $n \( E_parallel \, z \)$ at selected depths, (iii) total weighted distribution for tunneling calculation.
  ]
)
<fig_illustrationFN>

In a fully quantum-mechanical treatment, the inversion or accumulation layer at the semiconductor/oxide interface is described by the solutions ($Psi_i \, E_(parallel \, i)$) of the coupled Poisson-Schrodinger equation @invlayer_Stern1972@SPequation_nonuniform@pictureQBS_RegisterAPL1999@tunnelingmodel_Gehring. Each eigenstate $Psi_i$ contributes a two-dimensional sheet charge density $n_i \( E_(parallel \, i) \)$ at energy $E_(parallel \, i)$ that can tunnel through the oxide barrier @shinModelingCarrierTrapping2025. In a classical treatment, electrons are assumed to be plane waves that can tunnel through a barrie and the associated concentration is determined by the DOS. To marry these two viewpoints, we require an energy-resolved sheet density $n \( E_parallel \, E_perp \)$ from the 3D electron concentration $n \( E_parallel \, E_perp \, z \)$ in the space charge region where the influence of band-bending is retained via the local potential $phi \( z \)$. Therefore, we implement this mapping by the transformation $ n \( E_parallel \, E_perp \) & = integral_(z \( phi = 0 \))^(z \( phi_s \)) n \( E_parallel \, E_perp \, phi \) W_q \[ phi \( z \) \] upright(d) z \,\
n \( E_parallel \) & = integral_0^(E_perp) n \( E_parallel \, E_perp \) upright(d) E_perp $<eqn_eleconc4tunnel> where the weight function $W_(upright(q)) \[ phi \( z \) \]$ is a normalized weight that captures the fraction of the total surface charge arising from depth $z$. As illustrated in @fig_illustrationFN#text[a], $W_(upright(q)) \[ phi \( z \) \]$ is often built directly from the self-consistent Poisson solution under bias. The space-charge region is divided into thin slabs $\[ z_k \, z_k + Delta z_k \]$, and the incremental sheet-charge contributed by the slab at $z_k$ can be approximated as $ Delta Q_n \[ phi \( z_k \) \] approx {rho_e \[ phi \( z_k + Delta z_k \) \] - rho_e \[ phi \( z_k \) \]} z_k . $ in which the net negative space-charge density at $z$ is defined as $ rho_e \[ phi \( z \) \] = n \[ phi \( z \) \] - sum_(j = 0)^M N_(upright("d") \, j)^(+) \[ phi \( z \) \, j \] . $ where all $M$ distinctive dopants (sites) are considered. After normalization with respect to all $z_k$, the weight function is expressed as $ W_(upright(q)) \[ phi \( z_k \) \] = frac(Delta Q_n \[ phi \( z_k \) \], sum_k Delta Q_n \[ phi \( z_k \) \]) $ such that $sum_k W_(upright(q)) \( z_k \) = 1$. The $W_(upright(q))$ for a MOSC at $V_(upright("g")) = 40$V at 298 K is shown in @fig_illustrationFN#text[b], which suggests that only electrons that are 1-3 nm from the interface contribute significantly to the sheet charge.

After this mapping, the electrons incident on the oxide can be treated as a continuum of free-electrons parameterized by their longitudinal energy $E_parallel$ (along $z$) and transverse energy $E_perp$ (in-plane), i.e., $n \( E_parallel \, E_perp \)$. The concentration of electrons that actually tunnel into the CB of a-SiO$""_2$ is $ n_(upright("BTE")) \( E_parallel \, E_perp \) = n \( E_parallel \, E_perp \) D \( E_parallel \, E_perp \) $ where $D \( E_parallel \, E_perp \)$ is the tunneling coefficient. $n_(upright("BTE")) \( E_parallel \, E_perp \)$ serves as the initial condition for the subsequent transport and impact ionization in the CB of a-SiO$""_2$.

=== Tunneling coefficient
<tunneling-coefficient>
#figure(image("source/TunnelingModel_TunnelingCoeff.png", width: 100%),
  caption: [
    Methodology for calculating the tunneling coefficient. An electron is described as a plane wave $Psi_1 \( upright("z") \)$ and can tunnel into the CB at the classical turning-point $z_(upright("t,2"))$, near which its wave-function is denoted as $Psi_2 \( upright("z") \)$. The variable $upright("z")$ is defined as The potential $V \( upright("z") \)$ is referenced with respect to the CB of n-SiC. Note that $upright("z") = z_(upright("t,2")) - z$. The dashed olive (solid purple) curve depicts the $V \( upright("z") \)$ due to image-force correction (charge trapping in the oxide) alone. The largest tunneling distance is depicted as $z_(upright("t,m"))$.
  ]
)
<fig_TunnelingCoeff>

The methodology of calculating the tunneling coefficient $D \( E_parallel \, E_perp \)$ is illustrated in @fig_TunnelingCoeff, in which the variable z is defined as $upright("z") = z_(upright("t,2")) - z$. According to the mapping Eq. @eqn_eleconc4tunnel, electrons in the space-charge region can be modeled as plane-waves characterized by sheet density $n \( E_parallel \, E_perp \)$ and momentum $\( k_parallel \, k_perp \)$. The wave-function of these incident electrons penetrate into a-SiO$""_2$'s forbidden band gap, whose band-structure for electrons is modeled by a spherical parabolic band with effective mass $m_(upright("ox"))$, and eventually leak into the CB of a-SiO$""_2$ at the classical turning point $z_(upright("t") \, 2)$. The transmitted wave-function $Psi_2 \( upright("z") \)$ follow the Airy function with out-going characteristics for at least several nanometers. Denoting the complex magnitudes of the incident and transmitted wave by $A$ and $C_(upright("A"))$ respectively, we write the tunneling coefficient @tunnelingCoeff_FobesPRSA2011 $ D \( E_parallel \, E_perp \) = frac(\| C_(upright("A")) \/ sqrt(pi) \|^2, \| A \|^2) \, $<eqn_DExdef> which denote the probability ratio of these two waves. Here, the scalar $sqrt(pi)$ accounts for z-dependence of the modulus $\| Psi_2 \( upright("z") \) \|$. By matching the wave-functions at the SiC/a-SiO$""_2$ interface ($upright("z") = z_(upright("t,2"))$) according to the Ben Daniel-Duke boundary conditions @BenDanielDukecondition_PhyRev1966, we obtain the general tunneling coefficient as $  & D \( E_parallel \, E_perp \) =\
 & frac(4 pi^(- 1) omega^(- 1), 1 / omega [upright("Ai") \( xi \)^2 + upright("Bi") \( xi \)^2] + omega [upright("Ai")' \( xi \)^2 + upright("Bi")' \( xi \)^2] + 2 / pi) \,\
 & omega = frac(m_parallel xi \( upright("z") \)' #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\|]_(z_(upright("t,2"))), m_(upright("ox")) k_1) \, k_1 = sqrt(2 m_parallel) / ℏ sqrt(E_parallel (1 + alpha_parallel E_parallel)) $<eqn_TEx_full> in which the variable $xi \( E_parallel \, E_perp \)$ can be calculated as @bookAiryfunc_Olivier: $  & xi \( E_parallel \, E_perp \) = upright("sgn") \( upright("z") \) [3 / 2 Theta \( E_parallel \, E_perp \)]^(2 \/ 3) \,\
 & Theta \( E_parallel \, E_perp \) = integral_0^(z_(upright("t,2"))) k_(parallel \, upright("ox")) \( E_parallel \, E_perp \, upright("z") \) upright(d) upright("z") \,\
 & k_(parallel \, upright("ox")) \( E_parallel \, E_perp \, upright("z") \) = sqrt(2 m_(upright("ox"))) / ℏ times\
 & sqrt(lr(|V \( upright("z") \) - E_parallel - E_perp [1 - m_perp / m_(upright("ox")) (1 + alpha_perp E_perp)]|)) \,\
 & V \( upright("z") \) = E_(upright("c")) \( upright("z") \) + V_(upright("img")) \( upright("z") \) $<eqn_xi_kparallel_ox> Here, $E_(upright("c")) \( upright("z") \)$ is the CB edge of a-SiO$""_2$ and $V_(upright("img")) \( z \)$ the classical image-force potential; both are reference to the CB edge of SiC. The $E_perp$ term in $k_(parallel \, upright("ox"))$ emerges from the conservation of total energy and the conservation of $k_perp$ but without considering the mismatch of band structure at the SiC/a-SiO$""_2$ interface @tunnelingQBSPrefact_KriegerJAP1981@bandmismatch_WeinbergJAP1983.

For tunneling farther away from below the barrier maximum, the tunneling coefficient reads (Appendix @sec:APP_DExdeeptunnel) $ D \( E_parallel \, E_perp \) & = frac(omega^(- 1) times P_xi exp \[ - 2 Theta \( z_(upright("t,2")) \) \], 1 + 1 / 2 P_xi exp \[ - 2 Theta \( z_(upright("t,2")) \) \] + 1 / 4 exp \[ - 4 Theta \( z_(upright("t,2")) \) \]) \,\
P_xi & = frac(4 (k_1 \/ m_parallel) (k_(parallel \, upright("ox")) \/ m_(upright("ox"))), (k_1 \/ m_parallel)^2 + (k_(parallel \, upright("ox")) \/ m_(upright("ox")))^2) $<eqn_DExdeeptunnel> which is strongly influenced by the phase-shift $Theta \( z_(upright("t,2")) \)$. The formula from the Wentzel-Kramers-Brillouin (WKB) approximation is $ D \( E_parallel \, E_perp \)_(upright("WKB")) = omega^(- 1) exp \[ - 2 Theta \( z_(upright("t,2")) \) \] $<eqn_DExWKB> which completely neglects the pre-factor $P_xi$.

Since the $E_perp$-dependence of $D \( E_parallel \, E_perp \)$ mainly arises from the mismatch of the effective masses at the SiC/a-SiO$""_2$ interface, $D \( E_parallel \, E_perp \)$ can be factorized as $ D \( E_parallel \, E_perp \) = D \( E_parallel \, 0 \) D_perp \( E_parallel \, E_perp \) $ where $D \( E_parallel \, 0 \)$ is the zero-transverse-energy coefficient and $D_perp \( E_parallel \, E_perp \)$ contains the remaining variation with $E_perp$. Assuming electrons to follow a Maxwell-Boltzmann distribution with respect to $E_perp$, we obtain the averaged transverse-energy tunneling coefficient $ T_perp \( E_parallel \) = beta^(- 1) integral_0^oo D_perp \( E_parallel \, E_perp \) exp \( - beta E_perp \) upright(d) E_perp . $ Furthermore, the EDFs of electrons can be expressed with respect to $E_parallel$ as follows $ n_(upright("BTE")) \( E_parallel \) = n \( E_parallel \) D \( E_parallel \, 0 \) T_perp \( E_parallel \) $<eqn_nEiifortunnel> from which the initial EDF for the 1D BTE $n_(upright("BTE")) \( E \)$ can be obtained by properly converting $E_parallel$ to the total energy $E$.

=== Influence of charge-trapping and classical image-force correction
<influence-of-charge-trapping-and-classical-image-force-correction>
Classical image-force interaction and trapped charges in a-SiO$""_2$ change the tunneling potential $V \( upright("z") \)$ and thus alter the tunneling probability via $Theta \( E_parallel \, E_perp \)$, as illustrated in @fig_TunnelingCoeff. Trapped charges in a-SiO$""_2$ change the CB edge $E_(upright("c")) \( upright("z") \)$ according to Eq @eqn_dVox. The potential for classical image-force interaction $V_(upright("img")) \( z \)$ for thick oxide is approximated as $ V_(upright("img")) \( upright("z") \) = - frac(gamma_1, 4 \( z_(upright("t,2")) - upright("z") \)) \, gamma_1 = frac(1, 4 pi epsilon.alt_(upright("ox"))) frac(epsilon.alt_(upright("s")) - epsilon.alt_(upright("ox")), epsilon.alt_(upright("s")) + epsilon.alt_(upright("ox"))) $<eqn_Vimg> which deviates only slightly from the full solution @imageforceMOS_KleefstraJAP1980. Quantum mechanical corrections for the divergence at $upright("z") arrow.r z_(upright("t,2"))$ are not considered for $V_(upright("img")) \( upright("z") \)$ @Fischetti_shortcutpaperJAP1995. For thin oxide, Schenk and Heiser proposed a pseudopotential method for image-force correction with trapezoidal barriers @tunnelingthinoxide_Schenk, but is less relevant here for thick oxide.

The charge-trapping and image-force correction are decoupled in terms of their influence on the total potential $V \( upright("z") \)$. It is therefore feasible to simplify the calculation of the phase shift $Theta$ by approximating $E_(upright("c")) \( upright("z") \)$ properly. For a given $E_parallel$, the CB edge $E_(upright("c")) \( upright("z") \)$ can be approximated by connecting selected points on $E_(upright("c")) \( upright("z") \)$ with straight lines, for which the field constant. The zero$""^(upright("th"))$-order approximation to $E_(upright("c")) \( upright("z") \)$ is a straight line that connects $E_(upright("c")) \( upright("z") = z_(upright("t,2")) \)$ and $E_(upright("c")) \( upright("z") = 0 \)$. The first-order approximation to $E_(upright("c")) \( upright("z") \)$ are two straight lines, with the first one connecting $E_(upright("c")) \( upright("z") = z_(upright("t,2")) \)$ and $E_(upright("c")) \( upright("z") = upright("z")_(upright("c")) \)$, and the second one connecting $E_(upright("c")) \( upright("z") = upright("z")_(upright("c")) \)$ and $E_(upright("c")) \( upright("z") = 0 \)$ where $upright("z")_(upright("c")) in \( 0 \, z_(upright("t,2")) \)$. Hence, the total phase shift $Theta$ that incorporates the influence of charge-trapping and image-force correction can be calculated with the help of elliptical integrals. More details are given in Appendix @sec:APP_totalTheta.

== Carrier transport in a-SiO$""_2$
<sec:transport>
Carrier transport in amorphous a-SiO$""_2$ under high stress voltages involves coupled electron-hole dynamics with charge trapping and emission. To make the problem tractable, we assume quasi--steady-state conditions over short intervals: long enough for carrier scattering to equilibrate, yet short enough that trap occupation remains quasi-stationary. This decoupling is justified because electron flux is dominated by tunneling injection, which depends strongly on the electric field rather than small variations in capture or emission rates. The position-dependent EDFs of electrons are obtained by solving the BTE as the their high-energy tail govern impact ionization. In contrast, the hole current is calulated from a drift equation because their mobility is low and hol-initiated impact ionization is negligible. The following sections detail this framework, the treatment of impact ionization, and relaxation processes.

=== Boltzmann transport equation
<boltzmann-transport-equation>
In the 1D MOSC model, the external electric field is one-dimensional along $z$. The 3D transport of electrons is then projected onto the transport direction, making this transport problem 1D. Following @thermaltail_LacaitaAPL1991, we define the force $arrow(F_(upright("ox"))) \( z \) = - F_(upright("ox")) hat(z)$ where $F_(upright("ox"))$ is the potential gradient. If the CB of a-SiO$""_2$ follows a parabolic band ($E = frac(ℏ^2 k^2, 2 m_(upright("ox")))$), the simplified 1D BTE describes the evolution of the normalized EDF $n_(upright("BTE")) \( E \, z \)$ $ [F_(upright("ox")) \( z \) frac(partial, partial E) + frac(partial, partial z)] n_(upright("BTE")) \( E \, z \) = & - frac(n_(upright("BTE")) \( E \, z \) - n_(upright("eq")) \( E \, z \), lambda \( E \))\
 & + frac(S \( E \, z \), lambda \( E \)) $<eqn_BTE1d> where $lambda \( E \) = v_(upright("g")) \( E \) tau_(upright("m")) \( E \)$ represents the momentum relaxation length (MRL), $tau_(upright("m")) \( E \)$ the momentum relaxation time projected onto transport direction, and $v_(upright("g")) \( E \)$ the energy-dependent group velocity. The term $S \( E \, z \) \/ lambda \( E \)$ represents the generation of carriers due to impact ionization. Only the relaxation rate of electron-phonon scattering and impact ionization are considered for the solution of the 1D BTE. Electron-electron scattering is discarded because of the small electron concentration, while Coulomb scattering with charged impurities can be also ignored according to Ref. @offstoichSiO_DiMariaJAP1985@fischettiTheoryHighfieldElectron1985.

The 1D BTE is solved in time windows where $F_(upright("ox"))$ is frozen but updated after each interval to include trapped-charge effects. Within each window, the equation is integrated along the characteristics in the energy-coordinate plane, reducing it to an ordinary differential equation (ODE). The solution is advanced at discrete nodes using an operator-splitting approach: transport and relaxation are computed first without impact ionization, followed by carrier generation treated as ballistic with first-order kinetics. The two contributions are combined to obtain the EDF at the current node, which seeds the next step. This process is repeated across the domain and time steps. The numerical details are provided in Appendix @sec:App_1DBTE.

Key transport parameters are extracted from the EDF $n_(upright("BTE")) \( E \, z \)$. The average drift velocity along the transport direction is @adlerIntroductionSemiconductorPhysics1964@highFieldStatistics_BtinguierPRB1994: $ v_(upright("d")) \( z_i \) = 1 / 4 integral_0^oo n_(upright("BTE")) \( E \, z_i \) v_(upright("g")) \( E \) upright(d) E $<eqn_Jevg> where the factor $1 \/ 4$ accounts for flux projection @adlerIntroductionSemiconductorPhysics1964. Impact ionization is quantified by the impact ionization coefficient (II coefficient ) $ gamma_(upright("ox")) \( z_i \) = ln \[ 1 + delta n_(upright("BTE")) \( E \, z_i \) \] \/ delta z_i $ in which $delta n_(upright("BTE")) \( E \, z_i \)$ is the electron concentration generated in $\[ z_(i - 1) \, z_i \]$ . The II coefficient determines the amplification of electron current and the associated hole current in the oxide. The cumulative effect is expressed by the quantum yield, $ upright("QY") \( z \) = exp (integral_(z_(upright("t,m")))^z gamma_(upright("ox")) \( x \) upright(d) x) - 1 $ which represents the total number of electron-hole pairs generated between $z_(upright("t,m"))$ and $z$.

=== Impact ionization rate
<impact-ionization-rate>
#figure(image("source/IIRateStobbe.png", width: 94.0%),
  caption: [
    Comparison of the bulk impact ionization rate $w_(upright("II"))$ in a-SiO$""_2$ predicted by different models (Keldysh @transportMC_ArnoldPRB1994, modified Stobbe @5bandsaSiO2MC_Fitting, dielectricity theory @IIrate_Murat, and tetrahedral method calculation) against experimental deep inelastic scattering data @corelevel_CartierPRB1991. The modified Stobbe model, with parameters from Ref. @5bandsaSiO2MC_Fitting and adjusted $w_0$, shows excellent agreement with measurements and is adopted in this work.
  ]
)
<fig_IIrate>

The most widely used parameterization of the impact ionization rate is the Keldysh formula $ w_(upright("II")) \( E \) = w_0 (E / E_(upright("th")) - 1)^a \, E > E_(upright("th")) $ where $w_0$ and $a$ are fitting parameters ($a = 2$ for the classical form). However, this expression fails to reproduce the $1 \/ sqrt(E)$ scaling at high energies predicted by Beth's theory @Bethe_meanfreepath@meanfreepath_Ziaja. To address this limitation, Stobbe #emph[et al.] proposed a modifed form @STobbeIIrate_StobbePRB1991, later extended by Schreiber and Fitting for a-SiO$""_2$ @5bandsaSiO2MC_Fitting \$\$w\_\\text{II}(E) = w\_0\\left\\\[\\frac{E/E\_\\text{th}-1}{1 + D\\left(E/E\_\\text{th}\\right)^2}\\ln\\left(\\frac{E}{E\_\\text{th}}\\right)\\right\$\$^a,

EE\_<eqn_wIIStobbe> where $w_0$, $D$, and $a$ are fitted to experimental data. Alternative approaches include calculations based on the complex dielectric function @ashleyEnergyLossesMean1981@muratSpatialDistributionElectronhole2004, and first-principle methods using tetrahedral integration and realistic band structures @IIrateDFTMC_MizunoJAP .

@fig_IIrate compares these models with deep inelastic scattering measurements @corelevel_CartierPRB1991. In this work, we adopt the modified Stobbe model with parameters from Ref. @5bandsaSiO2MC_Fitting, adjusting $w_0$ to $2.45 times 10^15 upright(" s")^(- 1)$. This choice ensures both physical consistency and excellent agreement with expererimental data, making it well-suited for modeling impact ionization in a-SiO$""_2$.

=== Relaxation rate for electron-phonon scattering
<relaxation-rate-for-electron-phonon-scattering>
