<fig_structure>

To initiate the scattering analysis, we first construct a physically representative model of amorphous silicon dioxide (a-SiO$""_2$). Despite its lack of long-range order, a-SiO$""_2$ is approximated here as a periodic yet isotropic crystal, with a primitive cell containing a single SiO$""_2$ unit. This abstraction enables the use of a highly isotropic reciprocal space, where the Brillouin zone edge is defined by the Wigner-Seitz radius, denoted as $k_(upright("BZ"))$ (@fig_structure#[a(i)]). Using the bulk density of a-SiO$""_2$ (2.2 g$\/ upright("cm")^3$) @semiprocbookElKareh, we obtain $k_(upright("BZ")) = 10.93$ nm$""^(- 1)$ following the methodology of Ashlley #emph[et al.] @Acratecal_Ashley1987. This value aligns with prior estimates from Bradford #emph[et al.] (8.07 nm$""^(- 1)$) @Acinvlambda_BradfordJAP, DiMaria #emph[et al.] @VEballistic_DiMariaJAP1988 or Arnold #emph[et al.] @transportMC_ArnoldPRB1994 (12.08 nm$""^(- 1)$). For consistency, we adopt a one-dimensional $q$-space representation with $q_(upright("BZ")) = k_(upright("BZ"))$ (@fig_structure#[a(i)]).

Both electron and phonon dispersions are treated using the extended zone scheme. The CB of a-SiO$""_2$ is modeled as a spherical parabolic model with energy-dependent mass $m \( E \)$ @avelancheaSparks_PRB1981@Acratecal_Ashley1987@Acinvlambda_BradfordJAP@VEballistic_DiMariaJAP1988@transportMC_ArnoldPRB1994, expressed as: $ frac(m \( E \), m_0) = {m_(upright("eff,0")) \, & 0 lt.eq E < E_(upright("BZ")) / 2\
(2 m_(upright("eff,0")) - 1) + frac((1 - m_(upright("eff,0"))) E, 0.5 times E_(upright("BZ"))) \, & E_(upright("BZ")) / 2 lt.eq E < E_(upright("BZ"))\
1.0 \, & E gt.eq E_(upright("BZ")) $<eqn_meff_E> Here, $m_(upright("eff,0"))$ represents the band-edge effective mass (0.42 derived from tunneling mass @meffSiO2_MaserjianJVST1974@tunnelingthinoxide_Schenk), and Brillouin edge energy is given by $E_(upright("BZ")) = \( ℏ k_(upright("BZ")) \)^2 \/ 2 m_0 = 4.536$ eV, consistent with prior reports (5.52 eV @VEballistic_DiMariaJAP1988). The VB is modeled as three parabolic bands with identical energy maxima, and their effective masses are summarized in Table @tab:conduction_band. The resulting band structure captures key features of the group velocity $v_(upright("g")) \( E \)$ and the DOS from DFT calculations @TATpartII_Schleich2022, as illustrated in @fig_structure#text[b]. For energies below $0.5 E_(upright("BZ"))$, the parabolic behavior of $v_(upright("g")) \( E \)$ is consistent with observations in $beta$-cristobalite @vgDOSbCrystobalite_RudanPhysicaB and $alpha$-quartz @groupvelocityscintillators_HuangPSSRRL2016. To extend the description to higher energies, we propose an empirical formula $ frac(2 times 10^6 upright(" m/s"), v_(upright("g")) \( E \)) = frac(g_(upright("c") \, upright("DFT")) \( E \), 10^28 upright("m")^(- 3) upright("eV")^(- 1)) + frac(10^28 upright("m")^(- 3) upright("eV")^(- 1), g_(upright("c") \, upright("parab")) \( E \)) $<eqn_vg_parameterization> in which $g_(upright("c") \, upright("DFT")) \( E \)$ is the DOS from DFT and $g_(upright("c") \, upright("parab")) \( E \)$ is that of a parabolic band with energy-independent effective mass. The first term reflects the inverse relationship between $v_(upright("g"))$ and DOS at high energy, a behavior which is also observed in Si @MCsimIIvgDOS_Fischetti1991. Despite its simplicity, this empirical parameterization reproduces the $v_(upright("g")) \( E \)$ obtained from DFT with good accuracy and therefore provides a practical means to extrapolate $v_(upright("g")) \( E \)$ into the high-energy regime where neither DFT data nor experimental measurements are available.

The phonon dispersion in a-SiO$""_2$ is described by a 1D diatomic chain model (@fig_structure#text[c]). Acoustic phonons are approximated by a linear dispersion $ omega_(upright("AC")) \( q \) = {sqrt(2) / pi v_(upright("s")) q \, quad 0 lt.eq q < q_(upright("BZ"))\
sqrt(2) / pi v_(upright("s")) q_(upright("BZ")) \, quad q gt.eq q_(upright("BZ")) $<eq_acousticphonon> in which $v_(upright("s"))$ denotes the average sound velocity @avelancheaSparks_PRB1981@Acratecal_Ashley1987. This simplified form reproduces the results of the full 1D model, experimental data, and the DFT results @phononaSiOdft_Bosak@alphaQuartzphonon_StrauchJPCondMatter. Optical phonons exhibit flat dispersions $ omega_(upright("LO")) \( q \) = omega_(upright("LO")) \, omega_(upright("TO")) \( q \) = omega_(upright("TO")) $ for both longitudinal and transverse polarization. There are two dominant modes in each polarization, and their relative weights were determined from energy-loss functions @imqfuncaSiO2_GundePhysicaB2000 (@fig_structure#[c(ii)]). For LO phonons, large polarons will modify the mass of CB electrons @frohlichElectronsLatticeFields1954 and the modified band-edge mass $ m_p / m_0 approx frac(m_(upright("meff,0")), 1 - gamma_(upright("F")) \/ 6) $<eqn_meff_polaron> in which $gamma_(upright("F"))$ is the Frölich coupling constant.

Within the Born approximation, the scattering of an electron state $\| k chevron.r$ into state $\| k' chevron.r$ proceeds via absorption ("+") or emission ("$-$") of a phonon with wave-number $q$ and polarization $p$ from (or to) a quasi-stationary phonon bath. For an interaction Hamiltonian $H_(upright("ep"))$, time-dependent perturbation theory yields the transition rate @transporttheory_Lundstrom_2000@elphscatter_Ridley: $ w \( k \, k' \)_plus.minus = frac(2 pi, ℏ) \| V_p \|^2 I_(k \, k')^2 \| A_q \|^2 delta \[ E_(k') - E_k minus.plus ℏ omega \( q \) \] delta_(k plus.minus q - k' \, G) $<eqn_rate_wif> where $I_(k \, k') = chevron.l k' \| k chevron.r$ is the overlap integral, $\| V_p \|^2 = chevron.l k' \| H_(upright("ep")) \( q \) \| k chevron.r$ the squared matrix element for the electron-coordinate-dependent part of $H_(upright("ep"))$, and $A_q$ the phonon normal coordinate. The Dirac delta $delta \( E_(upright("f")) - E_(upright("i")) \)$ enforces energy conservation, while the Kronecker delta $delta_(k plus.minus q - k' \, G)$ ensure momentum conservation with $G$ a reciprocal lattice vector. Here, $G = 0$ suffices since Umklapp processes are implicitly included in the extended zone scheme. The phonon normal coordinate $A_q$ is given by @transporttheory_Lundstrom_2000 $ \| A_q \( plus.minus \) \|^2 = frac(ℏ {n_p [omega_p \( q \)] + 1 / 2 minus.plus 1 / 2}, 2 rho_(upright("c")) V_(upright("c")) omega_p \( q \)) \, $ where $V_(upright("c"))$ is the unit cell volume, $omega_p \( q \)$ the phonon angular frequency, and $ n_p \[ omega_p \( q \) \] = [exp \( beta ℏ omega_p \( q \) \) - 1]^(- 1) $<eqn_phonnumb> the corresponding phonon number.

We assume isotropic scattering, i.e., no explicit angular dependence. The momentum relaxation rate along the direction $hat(upright("F"))$ of an external field $upright(bold(F))$ is then given by @transporttheory_Lundstrom_2000@efffactor_WuPRB2015: $ w_m \( k \, k' \)_plus.minus = w \( k \, k' \)_plus.minus times (1 - k' / k cos theta) $<eqn_wm_kkp> where $theta$ is the scattering angle. The inverse momentum relaxation length (MRL) is $ lambda^(- 1) \( k \, k' \) = w_m \( k \, k' \) \/ v_k \( k \) $ with $v_k \( k \) = ℏ k \/ m \( k \)$ the velocity. The total momentum relaxation rate of an initial state $\| k chevron.r$ due to one mode $p$ of electron-phonon scattering follows from integrating $w_m \( k \, k' \)_plus.minus$ over $q$ @Llacer_LOintmethodJAP1969 $ w_m \( k \)_plus.minus = frac(V_(upright("c")), 2 pi ℏ) integral_(q_(upright("min"))^plus.minus)^(q_(upright("max"))^plus.minus) \| V_p \|^2 \| A_q \|^2 frac(q^2, v_k k) (frac(q, 2 k) minus.plus frac(omega_p \( q \), v_k q)) upright(d) q $<eq_totwm_atk> with integration limits determined by energy and momentum conservation $ q_(upright("max"))^plus.minus & = k [1 + sqrt(1 plus.minus ℏ omega_p \( q \) \/ E \( k \))]\
q_(upright("min"))^plus.minus & = k [minus.plus 1 plus.minus sqrt(1 plus.minus ℏ omega_p \( q \) \/ E \( k \))] $ Evaluation of Eq. @eq_totwm_atk requires explicit models for the electron band structure $E \( k \)$, the phonon dispersion $omega_p \( q \)$, and the interaction Hamiltonian $H_(upright("ep")) \( q \)$, which will be introduced shortly.

