Within deformation potential theory, the squared matrix element for scattering by acoustic (AC) phonons is @avelancheaSparks_PRB1981 $ \| V_p \|^2 = D_(upright("AC"))^2 q^2 $ where $D_(upright("AC"))$ is the deformation potential of a-SiO$""_2$ and is reckoned energy-independent. Following Bradford and Woolf @Acinvlambda_BradfordJAP and Ashley #emph[et al.] @Acratecal_Ashley1987, we adopt a modified squared matrix element $ \| V_p \|^2 & = frac(q^2 D_(upright("AC"))^2, [1 + \( q \/ q_alpha \)^2]^2) f_(upright("m")) \( q \)\
f_(upright("m")) \( q \) & = {1 + r_(upright("m")) \( q \/ k_(upright("BZ")) \)^2 \, quad q < k_(upright("BZ"))\
1 + r_(upright("m")) \, quad q gt.eq k_(upright("BZ")) $<eqn_Vasqrd_Ac> Here, the screened Coulomb potential term $1 \/ \[ 1 + \( q \/ q_alpha \)^2 \]$ dampens high-energy scattering beyond $E_(upright("bz"))$, while $f_(upright("m")) \( q \)$ accounts for smooth variation of scattering mass with energy. The constant $r_(upright("m"))$ represents the mass ratio between light and heavy constituents in the unit cell. The characteristic wave-vector $q_alpha$ is given by $ q_alpha = sqrt(frac(Z, kappa_oo epsilon.alt_0 V_(upright("c")) D_(upright("AC")))) $<eqn_qalpha_Ac> where $Z = 8$ corresponds to the dominant oxygen scatterer @Acinvlambda_BradfordJAP, and $kappa_oo$ is the high-frequency relative dielectric constant. For non-polar (transverse) optical phonons, the deformation potential theory @avelancheaSparks_PRB1981 yields an analogous form $ \| V_p \|^2 = D_(upright("TO"))^2 / [1 + \( q \/ q_alpha \)^2]^2 f_(upright("m")) \( q \) $ with $D_(upright("TO"))$ the deformation potential ($approx$ 200 eV/nm @statsTranspaSiO2_Ac3eV). Although some studies neglected phonon scattering @Unmklapp_ZakharovSSComm1988@transportMC_ArnoldPRB1994, we include it due to its demonstrated importance in high-field transport @statsTranspaSiO2_Ac3eV@VEballistic_DiMariaJAP1988. For longitudinal optical (LO) phonons, the Fröhlich formalism @transporttheory_Lundstrom_2000@elphscatter_Ridley applies $ \| V_p \|^2 = frac(omega \( q \)^2 rho_c, epsilon.alt_0 q^2) 1 / macron(kappa) \, quad 1 / macron(kappa) = 1 / kappa_oo - 1 / kappa_0 $ where $kappa_0$ is static dielectric constant. The quantity $1 \/ macron(kappa)$ can be extracted from the imaginary part of the dielectric function @Acratecal_Ashley1987.

The numerical values of the relevant parameters for AC, LO, and TO phonons used in this work are summarized in Table~@tab:scattering_param, together with their respective weights and references.

= Results and Discussions
<results-and-discussions>
== Electrostatics: comprehensive vs simplified approaches
<electrostatics-comprehensive-vs-simplified-approaches>
The electrostatics for n-SiC and n$""^(+)$-Si will be studied via a comprehensive model and a simplified model. The electrostatics of a-SiO$""_2$ will be discussed in sections focused on charge trapping. The comprehensive model uses Fermi-Dirac statistics for carriers, incomplete ionization models for dopants, and multiple Kane-type nonparabolic bands for CB (and VB) if applicable. The simplified model utilizes Boltzmann statistics for carriers, complete ionization model for dopants, and only one parabolic band for CB (and VB).

