<fig_electrostatics>

The temperature-dependent Fermi-level offsets ($E_(upright("CF"))$ and $E_(upright("FV"))$) and band offset shifts \[$Delta Phi_(upright("c")) \( T \)$ and $Delta Phi_(upright("v")) \( T \)$)\] are calculated according to the temperature dependent band-gap, and CB and VB edges. For example, band offset shifts for the SiC/a-SiO$""_2$ interface can be calculated as $ Phi_(upright("c")) \( T \) & = E_(upright("c")) \( T \, upright("SiO")_2 \) - E_(upright("c")) \( T \, upright("SiC") \) \,\
Phi_(upright("v")) \( T \) & = E_(upright("v")) \( T \, upright("SiC") \) - E_(upright("v")) \( T \, upright("SiO")_2 \) \,\
Delta Phi_(upright("c")) \( T \) & = Phi_(upright("c")) \( T \) - Phi_(upright("c")) \( 298 upright(" K") \) \,\
Delta Phi_(upright("v")) \( T \) & = Phi_(upright("v")) \( T \) - Phi_(upright("c")) \( 298 upright(" K") \) . $ The results are shown in @fig_Tdepbarrier for an n-SiC/SiO$""_2$/n$""^(+)$-Si MOSC in the temperature range from 80 K to 573 K. The first striking observation is that Fermi-level offset $E_(upright("CF")) \( T \)$ and band offset shift $Delta Phi_(upright("c")) \( T \)$ show the opposite temperature dependency, above room temperature. This fact will help to resolve the controversy regarding the potential barrier for electron tunneling, i.e., whether it is the band offset $Phi_(upright("c")) \( T \)$ or the effective barrier defined as $ Phi_(upright("eff")) \( T \) = Phi_(upright("c")) \( T \) + E_(upright("CF")) \( T \) $ as illustrated in @fig_TunnelingCoeff. The barrier $Phi_(upright("eff")) \( T \)$ \[$Phi_(upright("c")) \( T \)$\] increases (decreases) with increasing temperature, and hence result in smaller (largers) gate leakage current. A detailed discussion will be given in later sections. The second observation is that the comprehensive approach predicts a smaller range of $E_(upright("CF")) \( T \)$ than the simplified approach does. Since the Fermi-level offset will significantly influence the concentration of free carriers (eq. @eqn_elhconc), it is thus necessary to use the comprehensive approach for electrostatics.

We analyzed the electrostatics of a 1D MOSC under gate bias $V_(upright("g"))$ from 0 to 52 V at 80, 298, and 573 K using both the comprehensive and the simplified approaches, as shown in @fig_electrostatics. At a given $V_(upright("g"))$, the comprehensive approach consistently predicts higher surface potentials ($psi_(upright("s"))$ and $psi_(upright("g"))$) and lower charge densities ($\| Q_(upright("s")) \|$, $\| Q_(upright("g")) \|$) than the simplified approach does, as shown in @fig_electrostatics#text[a-b]. The difference between both approaches become particularly significant when the Fermi level starts crossing the band edges, which happens at $V_(upright("g")) approx 10$ V (27 V) for SiC (n$""^(+)$-Si) at 298 K. The spatial distributions of carriers and ionized dopants are shown in @fig_electrostatics#text[c]. The comprehensive approach shows incomplete ionization effects, in the n-SiC near the SiC/SiO$""_2$ interface, which becomes more pronounced at lower temperatures, and negligible incomplete ionization for the degenerate n$""^(+)$-Si gate. The simplified approach predicts that concentration of free carrier is more concentrated near the interface. The carrier concentrations as a function of $E_parallel$ at 1 nm from the respective interfaces are shown in @fig_electrostatics#text[d] for $V_(upright("g")) = 40$ V. The comprehensive model shows larger concentration at higher energies and the contribution of sub-bands of CB and VB. These carrier concentration will enhance the gate leakage current due to smaller tunneling barriers. A more detailed analysis will be given in later sections.

Interface charges, whether pre-existing or generated by hot carriers under gate leakage stress, can alter global electrostatics (Eq. @eqns_surfpot) and locally perturb the field near the interfaces. Their impact is more pronounced on the lightly doped SiC side, where modest interface charge can significantly modify the anode-side potential and influence tunneling and trapping. In contrast, the heavily doped gate maintains large surface charge (@fig_electrostatics#text[a]) and thus screens electrostatic shifts. Without explicit modeling or experimental input, the quantitative influence of these traps remains uncertain.

== Gate leakage current due to tunneling
<gate-leakage-current-due-to-tunneling>
In this section, the gate leakage current due to tunneling will be simulated without considering the effect of impact ionization and later compared with experimental observation. According to the “tunneling + transport\" procedure, the gate leakage current without impact ionization can be calculated as $ J_(upright("g")) = v_(upright("d")) \( L \) n_(upright("FN")) \( z_(upright("t,m")) \) $<eqn_JgFNalone> in which $n_(upright("FN")) \( z_(upright("t,m")) \) = integral n_(upright("FN")) \( E \) upright(d) E$ is the total electron concentration that has tunneled into the CB of a-SiO$""_2$ and $v_(upright("d")) \( L \)$ is the averaged drift velocity at the n$""^(+)$-Si/a-SiO$""_2$ interface. We adopt $v_d = 1.52 times 10^5$ m/s for all temperatures and field strengths, which is supported by experimental results @driftvelocity_HughesPRL1973. The influence of the classical image-force and charge trapping in a-SiO$""_2$ directly change the current $J_(upright("g"))$ via changing the electron concentration $n_(upright("FN")) \( z_(upright("t,m")) \)$.

According to the conventional Tsu-Esaki formalism together with the WKB formula for tunneling coefficient @Tunneling_TsuEsaki@WKBTdeptunnel_Fromhold@tunnelingmodel_Gehring, the gate leakage current $J_(upright("g"))$ can be calculated according to the Lenzlinger-Snow formula @tunneling_LenzlingerSnowJAP1969 $ J_(upright("g")) (F_(upright("ox"))) = & frac(F_(upright("ox"))^2 exp [- 2 Theta_0 \( F_(upright("ox")) \) v \( y \)] m_(upright("d")), \( 4 pi \)^2 ℏ Phi_(upright("eff")) \( T \) m_(upright("ox")))\
 & times {t \( y \)^2 upright("sinc") [2 Theta_0 \( F_(upright("ox")) \) t \( y \) frac(3 k_(upright("B")) T \/ 2, Phi_(upright("eff")) \( T \))]}^(- 1)\
Theta_0 \( F_(upright("ox")) \) = & 2 / 3 frac(sqrt(2 m_(upright("ox")) Phi_(upright("eff")) \( T \)^3), ℏ F_(upright("ox"))) $<eqn_JgLS> where $m_(upright("d"))$ denotes the DOS effective mass @tunnelingmodel_Gehring and $Phi_(upright("eff")) \( T \)$ is the barrier height. Here, the classical image-force effects enter through the auxilliary functions $v \( y \)$ and $t \( y \)$ @approxvy_ForbesAPL2006, respectively $  & v \( y \) approx 1 - y^2 \[ 1 - ln \( y \) \/ 3 \] \,\
 & t \( y \) approx 1 + (y / 3)^2 [1 - ln \( y \)] $ in which $y = frac(sqrt(\| gamma_1 F_(upright("ox")) \|), Phi_(upright("eff")) \( T \) - E_parallel)$. The barrier height $Phi_(upright("eff")) \( T \)$ increases and thus the dominant part of the tunneling coefficient $exp \[ - 2 Theta_0 \( F_(upright("ox")) \) v \( y \) \]$ decreases with increasing temperature. This predicts a negative correlation between temperature and $J_(upright("g"))$ at a given gate bias $V_(upright("g"))$.

