We calculate the gate leakage current $J_(upright("g"))$ at 298 K using both Eq. @eqn_JgFNalone (full model) and Eq. @eqn_JgLS (LS model), without including electron/hole traps nor impact ionization. The effect of classical image force correction has also been examined. The features of the gate leakage current are summarized in @fig_JgVgT298K. In @fig_JgVgT298K#text[a], the full model without image-force correction yields an excellent agreement with the experimental gate leakage current $J_(upright("g"))$, while the image-force correction enhances $J_(upright("g"))$ by a factor of about 2. The LS model without image-force correction predicts $J_(upright("g"))$ that is about 5 times smaller than the one from the full model. Hence, the full model not only predicts better $J_(upright("g"))$ but also provides $n_(upright("FN")) \( E \)$ as an initial condition for the 1D BTE, for which the LS model cannot be used. As shown in @fig_JgVgT298K#text[b-c], the second sub-band of the CB of n-SiC contributes around 15% of the total $J_(upright("g"))$, and the EDFs of tunneled elctrons associated with this sub-band show highly energetic electrons ($E > 0.115$ eV) regardless of the field $F_(upright("ox"))$. The tail of these EDFs reflects a Boltzmann distribution because of the termination of the sub-bands of the CB of SiC (\[001\]). Hence it is evident that the full model should be used to calculate gate leakage currents.

