    Temperature-dependent gate leakage characteristics. (a) Experimental (triangles) and calculated (lines) $J_(upright("g")) \( V_(upright("g")) \)$ curves for temperatures $T_i = 80 \, 135 \, dots.h.c \, 573$ K ($i = 0 \, 1 \, dots.h.c \, 9$), each scaled by $50^i$ for clarity. Arrows indicate gate voltages corresponding to fixed fields $F_(upright("ox")) = 5.0 upright("-") 9.0$ MV/cm. (b) Linear relationship between $ln \( J_(upright("g")) \/ F_(upright("ox"))^2 \)$ and $Phi_(upright("c")) \( T \)^(3 \/ 2)$ at constant fields. (c) Field dependence of linear regression parameters: (i) slope $p$ and (ii) intercept $s$ versus $1 \/ F_(upright("ox"))$.
  ]
)
<fig_JgVgTdepAll>

@fig_JgVgTdepAll summarizes the temperature dependence of gate leakage current $J_(upright("g"))$ calculated by using the full model without image-force correction. Good agreement was achieved between the experimental observation and the simulated value for temperatures from 80 K to 573 K, as shown in @fig_JgVgTdepAll#text[a] where the $J_(upright("g"))$-$V_(upright("g"))$ curves were scaled for clarity. The abrupt current amplification of $J_(upright("g"))$ at larger $V_(upright("g"))$ stems from impact ionization and hole capture in a-SiO$""_2$, which will be covered in the following sections.

As shown in @fig_JgVgTdepAll#text[b], the $J_(upright("g"))$ values at fixed fields $F_(upright("ox")) = 5.0 \, 6.0 \, 7.0 \, 8.0 \, 9.0$ MV/cm (corresponding gate voltages marked by arrows in @fig_JgVgTdepAll#text[b]) reveal a linear relationship between $ln \( J_(upright("g")) \/ F_(upright("ox"))^2 \)$ and $Phi_(upright("c")) \( T \)^(3 \/ 2)$, with its slope $p$ and intercept $s$ shown in @fig_JgVgTdepAll#text[c]. Both experiment and simulation reveal that $J_(upright("g")) \( F_(upright("ox")) \)$ increases with increasing temperature because $Phi_(upright("c")) \( T \)$ decreases with increasing temperature. This indicates $Phi_(upright("c")) \( T \)$ is the barrier height for tunneling rather than $Phi_(upright("eff")) \( T \)$ as conventionally assumed in the Lenzlinger-Snow formalism. The linear dependency of the slope $s$ (intercept) on $1 \/ F_(upright("ox"))$ can be readily understood from the phase-shift Eq. @eqn_JgLS.

The fact that $Phi_(upright("c")) \( T \)$ is the barrier height for tunneling is crucial for higher sub-bands of the CB of SiC. The barrier height will be overestimated if $Phi_(upright("eff")) \( T \)$ is used as the barrier height because the Fermi-level offset $E_(upright("CF"))$ will be added by the min-gap of the two sub-bands.

== Electron transport in a-SiO$""_2$
<electron-transport-in-a-sio_2>
The features of electron transport in the CB of a-SiO$""_2$ and the electron-initiated impact ionization will be discussed in this section. At first, the inverse mean relaxation lengths (MRLs) $lambda^(- 1) \( E \)$ due to electron-phonon scattering will be calculated using methods introduced in previous sections. Then, the 1d BTE will be solved for the 1D MOSC, to determine the EDFs of electrons, the average drift velocity, averaged energy, II coefficient and QY for electron-hole pair generation. The dependency of these quantities on temperature and/or electric field yield well-known laws for impact ionization.

=== The inverse mean relaxation length
<the-inverse-mean-relaxation-length>
#figure(image("source/elphIIRateAll.png", width: 96.0%), scope: "parent", placement: top,
  caption: [
    Electron-phonon scattering in a-SiO$""_2$ expressed via the inverse mean relaxation length $lambda^(- 1) \( E \, T \)$. (a) Contributions from acoustic (AC), longitudinal optical (LO), and transverse optical (TO) phonons at 298 K, compared with DFT predictions for $beta$-cristobalite @vgDOSbCrystobalite_RudanPhysicaB and experimental measurements @Expinvlambda_CartierPhysScriptaT23. The $lambda^(- 1) \( E \, T \)$ for II is displayed for comparison. (b) Temperature evolution of $lambda^(- 1) \( E \, T \)$ from 80 K to 573 K, with arrows marking energies selected for detailed temperature analysis. (c) Temperature dependence at selected energies fitted to asymptotic formulae with characteristic phonon energies $ℏ omega$.
  ]
)
<fig_elphIIrate>

The inverse mean relaxation lengths (MRLs) $lambda^(- 1) \( E \)$ were calculated using parameters from Table @tab:scattering_param for all types of electron-phonon scattering mechanisms. The results at 298 K are shown in @fig_elphIIrate#text[a] and compared with DFT results for $beta$-cristobalite @vgDOSbCrystobalite_RudanPhysicaB which were converted from scattering rates $w \( E \)$ and group velocity $v_(upright("g")) \( E \)$ according to $ lambda^(- 1) \( E \) = 0.42 / 0.68 frac(w \( E \), v_(upright("g")) \( E \)) $ where the factor 0.42/0.68 accounts for the difference of the effective mass between $beta$-cristobalite and a-SiO$""_2$. The calculated total $lambda^(- 1) \( E \)$ shows excellent agreement with both DFT results @vgDOSbCrystobalite_RudanPhysicaB and experimental measurements @Expinvlambda_CartierPhysScriptaT23. Notably, phonon contributions become negligible above $tilde.op 1.5$ eV, challenging previous interpretations that experimental rates can be decomposed solely into and phonon contributions @Expinvlambda_CartierPhysScriptaT23@corelevel_CartierPRB1991. In the energy range $\( E_(upright("BZ")) \/ 2 \, E_(upright("BZ")) \)$, both and phonons contribute to a sharp increase in total $lambda^(- 1)$, primarily due to effective mass enhancement (Eq. @eqn_meff_E). Beyond $E_(upright("BZ"))$, the total $lambda^(- 1)$ decays as $E^(- 1)$, mainly due to phonons, and yield $E^(- 1 \/ 2)$ law of set by Arnold #emph[et al.] @transportMC_ArnoldPRB1994.

The inverse MRL results in @fig_elphIIrate#text[a] reveal significant implications for high-field transport. Electron-phonon scattering alone can stabilize the electron distribution function (EDF), with maxima at $tilde.op 153$ meV and $tilde.op 5.0$ eV from LO and AC phonons respectively preventing “run-away\" phenomena @driftvelocity_FerryJAP1979. Furthermore, additional relaxation mechanisms, particularly impact ionization, can provide extra stabilization channels beyond the impact ionization threshold $E_(upright("th"))$, potentially enabling higher breakdown fields.

@fig_elphIIrate#text[b] illustrates the temperature dependence of total $lambda^(- 1) \( E \)$ from 80 to 573 K. Higher temperatures yield larger $lambda^(- 1) \( E \)$ values, with increased curvature below $tilde.op 30$ meV. The temperature dependent $lambda^(- 1) \( E \)$ were extracted for several chosen energies (as indicated by arrows in @fig_elphIIrate#text[b]) and the results are shown in @fig_elphIIrate#text[c]. The temperature dependence exhibits distinct characteristics at different energies. At 5250 meV (\> $E_(upright("BZ"))$), the behavior follows $ lambda^(- 1) \( T \, E \) = 0.5 lambda_0^(- 1) \( E \) upright("coth") (0.5 beta ℏ omega) $<eqn_invlambdaTdepAc> with $ℏ omega = 13$ meV, indicating a dominance of phonons. Near 86 meV, the dependence follows $ lambda^(- 1) \( E \, T \) = lambda_0^(- 1) \( E \) n_p \[ omega_p \( q \) \] $<eqn_invlambdaTdepOpt> with $ℏ omega = 63$ meV above 200 K, revealing phonon dominance with possible contributions of phonons at lower temperatures. The behavior at 6 meV shows a transition from characteristics ($ℏ omega = 6$ meV) at low temperatures to behavior ($ℏ omega = 63$ meV) at high temperatures. Interestingly, at 204 meV, $lambda^(- 1)$ varies almost linearly with temperature, reflecting the combined influence of , , and contributions. These varied temperature dependencies demonstrate that $lambda^(- 1) \( E \)$ cannot be simply scaled from room temperature values but must be calculated directly for each temperature.

=== Electron transport
<electron-transport>
#figure(image("source/T298IIFoxdep.png", width: 96.0%), scope: "parent", placement: top,
  caption: [
    Electron transport characteristics in a-SiO$""_2$ under a constant field $F_(upright("ox"))$ at 298 K. (a) Spatial evolution of transport parameters versus distance $z$ from the SiC/a-SiO$""_2$ interface: (i) II coefficient $gamma_(upright("ox")) \( z \)$, (ii) quantum yield $upright("QY") \( z \)$, (iii) average drift velocity $v_d \( z \)$, and (iv) average electron energy $chevron.l E chevron.r \( z \)$ for different field strengths. Note: $gamma_(upright("ox"))$ and $upright("QY") \( z \)$ values for 9.0 MV/cm and 7.0 MV/cm are scaled by 0.075 and 750 respectively for clarity. The shaded area indicates the dark space region. (b) Normalized EDFs $n_(upright("BTE")) \( E \, z \)$ for fields of (i) 5.0 MV/cm, (ii) 7.0 MV/cm, and (iii) 9.0 MV/cm at selected distance $z$, compared with Monte Carlo results @transportMC_ArnoldPRB1994 and experimental data from DiMaria #emph[et al.] @trapcreatation_DiMariaJAP1989 and Brorson #emph[et al.] @EDFsdirectmeas_BrorsonJAP1985. (c) Field dependence of transport parameters at $z = 52$ nm: (i) II coefficient $gamma_(upright("ox"))$ and (ii) quantum yield $upright("QY")$ compared with experimental data @transportMC_ArnoldPRB1994@IIcoefaSiO2_Solomon, showing agreement with the Chynoweth model @IIcoef_Chynoweth1958\; (iii) average drift velocity $v_(upright("d"))$ compared with experiments @driftvelocity_HughesPRL1973 and Ferry's calculations @driftvelocity_FerryJAP1979\; (iv) average energy $chevron.l E chevron.r$ compared with experimental measurements @transportMC_ArnoldPRB1994 and DiMaria's Monte Carlo simulations with and without quantum effects @EavgthrdMCsim_DiMaria.
  ]
)
<fig_IIFoxdep>

We begin our discussion on electron transport in a-SiO$""_2$ by solving the 1D BTE under uniform electric fields. The solution provides the electron's EDF $n_(upright("BTE")) \( E \, z \)$ and enables extraction of the average drift velocity $v_(upright("d")) \( z \)$, average electron energy $chevron.l E chevron.r \( z \)$, II coefficient $gamma_(upright("ox")) \( z \)$, and quantum yield $upright("QY") \( z \)$ as a function of oxide field $F_(upright("ox"))$.

@fig_IIFoxdep presents results at 298 K for fields ranging from 1.0 to 11.0 MV/cm. The spatial evolution of transport characteristics at field strengths of 5.0, 7.0, and 9.0 MV/cm is given in @fig_IIFoxdep#text[a]. Taking $F_(upright("ox")) = 7.0 upright(" MV/cm")$ as an example, electron transport starts at $z_(upright("t,m"))$, but $gamma_(upright("ox")) \( z \)$ remains negligible upto a field-dependent distance $l_(upright("d"))$, beyond which it rises sharply and then stabilizes with minor variations. This behavior demonstrates the “dark space\" concept @transportMC_ArnoldPRB1994, where electrons must traverse a minimum distance to accumulate energy from the field before being able to initiate impact ionization. Hence, the quantum yield $upright("QY") \( z \)$ increases significantly only beyond this “dark space\". Both average drift velocity $v_(upright("d")) \( z \)$ and average energy $chevron.l E chevron.r \( z \)$ saturate approximately 5 nm beyond $z_(upright("t,m"))$. Interestingly, $chevron.l E chevron.r \( z \)$ shows a linear relationship with $z$ within $3 tilde.op 4$ nm after $z_(upright("t,m"))$ and thus exhibits near-ballistic behavior, which is consistent with experimental observations @ballistictransportaSiO2_DiMariaPRL1986.

The EDFs at different field strengths (@fig_IIFoxdep#text[b]) reveal crucial details about the transport mechanisms. For $F_(upright("ox")) = 7.0 upright(" MV/cm")$ ($V_(upright("g")) approx 40$ V), the initial EDF at $z_(upright("t,m")) approx 3.9$ nm peaks around $E approx 0.1$ eV. After 13 nm transport ($z approx 17$ nm), the EDF develops a significant tail above the impact ionization threshold $E_(upright("th"))$ ($tilde.op 8.7$ eV at 298 K). Hence, the “dark space\" phenomenon originate from a simple fact that carriers must transverse sufficient distance to develop high-energy tails to trigger substantial impact ionization. Experimental EDFs from DiMaria et al. @trapcreatation_DiMariaJAP1989 and Brorson #emph[et al.] @EDFsdirectmeas_BrorsonJAP1985 show a secondary high-energy tail at energy between 5.0 and 8.0 eV. While Fischetti attributed this to the DOS of a-SiO$""_2$ and quantum mechanical effects @fischettiQuantumMC_PRL552475, Bradford and Woolf suggested it results from mixing of EDFs caused by the pinholes in the aluminum contact of the MOSC studied @gateFilter_Bradford01091991. Beyond 9 eV, the EDFs decrease with energy because of relaxation due to impact ionization and the EDFs can thus stabilize. The presence of ballistic transport is evidenced by the spikes in these EDFs.

@fig_IIFoxdep#text[c] presents the field dependence of the transport parameters at $z = 52$ nm, which is consistent with that from Anorld @transportMC_ArnoldPRB1994 and DiMaria @EavgthrdMCsim_DiMaria for comparison. The calculated II coefficient $gamma_(upright("ox")) \( F_(upright("ox")) \)$ under homogeneous fields shows good agreement with the results from previous studies @transportMC_ArnoldPRB1994@IIcoefaSiO2_Solomon, and it follows the Chynoweth formula @IIcoef_Chynoweth1958: $ gamma_(upright("ox")) \( F_(upright("ox")) \) = a exp (- b \/ F_(upright("ox"))) $<eqn_Chynoweth> where the constants $a$ and $b$ vary with temperature. The simulated quantum yield $upright("QY") \( F_(upright("ox")) \)$, which is typically the primary experimental observable from which $gamma_(upright("ox"))$ is derived, matches well with carrier-separation measurements from Si FETs of comparable oxide thickness @transportMC_ArnoldPRB1994. Contrary to the assumption employed by Arnold #emph[et al.] @transportMC_ArnoldPRB1994, we believe that the $upright("QY") \( F_(upright("ox")) \)$ measured at lower field is more reliable because other channels of injecting holes into the VB of a-SiO$""_2$, such as anode hole injection @DiMariaAnodeholeinjection_JAP80304, can be active under high fields. The average drift velocity $v_(upright("d")) \( F_(upright("ox")) \)$ initially exhibits a field-dependent growth and then saturates at approximately $1.52 times 10^5$ m/s. This value is consistent with experimental measurements at fields of about 1.0 MV/cm @driftvelocity_HughesPRL1973. For higher fields, where experimental data is unavailable, our results align more closely with Ferry's uncorrected Monte Carlo simulations ('cal. 2, Ferry' in @fig_IIFoxdep#text[c-iii]) than with his field-modified scattering calculations ('cal. 1, Ferry' in @fig_IIFoxdep#text[c-iii]) @driftvelocity_FerryJAP1979. The calculated average energy $chevron.l E chevron.r \( F_(upright("ox")) \)$ demonstrates a good agreement with DiMaria's quantum-corrected calculations ('sim., DiMaria (e)' in @fig_IIFoxdep#text[c-iv]) @EavgthrdMCsim_DiMaria and experimental data from 100 nm oxide measurements @transportMC_ArnoldPRB1994. Intriguingly, DiMaria's uncorrected calculations ('sim., DiMaria (d)' in @fig_IIFoxdep#text[c-iv]) show better agreement with experiments, underscoring the complexity of high-field electron transport phenomena.

#figure(image("source/TdeptransportAll.png", width: 96.0%), scope: "parent", placement: top,
  caption: [
    Temperature-dependent electron transport in a-SiO$""_2$ under uniform fields. (a) Spatial evolution at $F_(upright("ox")) = 7.0$ MV/cm showing temperature dependence of: (i) II coefficient $gamma_(upright("ox")) \( z \)$, (ii) quantum yield $upright("QY") \( z \)$, (iii) average drift velocity $v_(upright("d")) \( z \)$, and (iv) average electron energy $chevron.l E chevron.r \( z \)$. Note: the $gamma_(upright("ox"))$ and $upright("QY") \( z \)$ values at 80 K and 573 K are scaled by 5$times 10^(- 4)$ and 2$times 10^4$ respectively. (b) Normalized EDFs at $z = 52$ nm for temperatures 80, 298, and 573 K at fields: (i) 5.0 MV/cm, (ii) 7.0 MV/cm, and (iii) 9.0 MV/cm. (c) Temperature effects on: (i) field-dependent II coefficient across 80-573 K following Chynoweth's law, (ii) temperature dependence of $gamma_(upright("ox"))$ at various fields following the model of Okuto and Crowell @okutoThresholdEnergyEffect1975, and (iii) field-dependent drift velocity across the temperature range.
  ]
)
<fig_IITdep>

The temperature dependence of electron transport, illustrated in @fig_IITdep, reveals significant variations across multiple parameters. At $F_(upright("ox")) =$ 7.0 MV/cm, both impact ionization coefficient $gamma_(upright("ox")) \( z \, F_(upright("ox")) \)$ and quantum yield $upright("QY") \( z \, F_(upright("ox")) \)$ exhibit a strong temperature sensitivity, spanning eight orders of magnitude between 80 K and 573 K. The drift velocity $v_(upright("d")) \( z \)$ and average energy $chevron.l E chevron.r \( z \)$ show pronounced spatial oscillations with reduced damping at lower temperatures, likely due to increased mean relaxation lengths. The EDFs at $z = 52$ nm (@fig_IITdep#text[b]) become broader at lower temperatures for all field strengths (5.0, 7.0, and 9.0 MV/cm). This broadening reflects reduced scattering at lower temperatures, allowing electrons to reach higher energies due to smaller inverse MRLs $lambda^(- 1) \( E \, T \)$. The field-dependent II coefficient $gamma_(upright("ox")) \( F_(upright("ox")) \, T \)$ follows the Chynoweth's law @IIcoef_Chynoweth1958 (Eq. @eqn_Chynoweth) across all temperatures, with temperature-dependent parameter $b$. The temperature dependence of $gamma_(upright("ox"))$ follows the model of Okuto and Crowell @okutoThresholdEnergyEffect1975: $ gamma_(upright("ox")) \( F_(upright("ox")) \, T \) & = a_(upright("ref")) [1 + c \( T - T_(upright("ref")) \)] F_(upright("ox"))\
 & times exp [- (frac(b_(upright("ref")) {1 + d \( T - T_(upright("ref")) \)}, F_(upright("ox"))))^2] $<eqn_gammaoxTdep> where $a_(upright("ref"))$ and $b_(upright("ref"))$ are parameters at a reference temperature (typically $T_(upright("ref")) = 300$ K), and $c$, $d$ are fitting parameters independent of temeprature. For semiconductor-like behavior ($c \, d tilde.op 10^(- 4)$), this simplifies to: $ ln \( gamma_(upright("ox")) \) prop - (b_(upright("ref")) / F_(upright("ox")))^2 [1 + 2 d \( T - T_(upright("ref")) \)] $<eqn_gammaoxTdepsimple> producing linear $ln \( gamma_(upright("ox")) \)$-$T$ relationships with slopes proportional to $1 \/ F_(upright("ox"))^2$, as confirmed in @fig_IITdep#[c-(ii)]. Quadratic corrections become significant only at high fields and low temperatures. The drift velocity $v_(upright("d")) \( F_(upright("ox")) \, T \)$ shows substantial temperature dependence, exceeding or falling below the room-temperature value of 1.52$times 10^5$ m/s at low and high temperatures, respectively.

This analysis demonstrates that our simplified 1D BTE model, which incorporates electron-phonon scattering and impact ionization, successfully captures the essential features of temperature-dependent electron transport in a-SiO$""_2$.

== Hole trapping in a-SiO$""_2$ 
<sec:charge_capture>
The generation of electron-hole pairs due to impact ionization in a-SiO$""_2$ will lead to hole current in the VB and subsequent hole capture into traps in the entire insulator. The hole capture can be described by the MT model which incorporates the phonon-assisted tunneling as well. The parameters for the NMP model and MT model are the following: $c_0 = 9.83$, $macron(N_(upright("p"))) = 10^25$ s$""^(- 1)$ m$""^(- 2)$, and $ℏ omega = 63$ meV (in previous sections). One acceptor-like trap-band and two donor-like trap-bands were used and their parameters were listed in Table @tab:holetrap_parameters. The trap levels for the donor-like traps are consistent with the experimental ones @holetrapband_CaiJAP83_851, and are likely due to oxygen vacancies (cite dft result). and the cross-sections for hole capture are close to those in Ref. @holemobiaSiO2_HughesAPL264362@wagerLowfieldTransportSiO22017. The parameters of the acceptor-like traps are close to those in Ref. @SchleichIEDM19 where these traps were proven to be responsible for the BTI in SiC MOSFETs.

