The gate leakage current $J_(upright("g"))$ during a $V_(upright("g"))$ sweep at 298 K was simulated with the MT model, and compared with the $J_(upright("g"))$ simulated by considering the FN tunneling alone. The results are presented in @fig_T298Khump. $J_(upright("g"))$ from the FN tunneling mechanism alone provides a reference which is free from the influence of trapped charges in the oxide. As shown in @fig_T298Khump#[a(i)], the MT model quantitatively predicts the abrupt enhancement of $J_(upright("g"))$ at $V_(upright("g")) approx 47$ V, which is absent in the the FN tunneling mechanism. When $V_(upright("g"))$ exceeds around 47 V, $J_(upright("g")) \( V_(upright("g")) \)$ predicted by the MT model differs only by a scalar factor from that obtained from the FN tunneling model. This indicates that capture of holes into a-SiO$""_2$ dominantly took place when $V_(upright("g"))$ was swept from 45 to 47 V and that this capture is almost saturated when $V_(upright("g")) > 47$ V. Such an evolution of trapped holes can be also understood from the shift of flat-band voltage $Delta V_(upright("fb"))$ from the simulation shown in the (ii) panel of @fig_T298Khump#text[a]. The magnitude of $Delta V_(upright("fb"))$ is consistent with experimental observations for SiC MOSFETs @avramenkoThresholdVoltageDrift2024. The electric field near the SiC/a-SiO$""_2$ interface was thus enhanced in a narrow $V_(upright("g"))$ window, leading to an abrupt enhancement (or “hump\") of $J_(upright("g"))$.

We define a critical field $F_(upright("cr"))$ characterizing the onset of hole capture and thus the onset of the “hump\". A further study of the temperature dependence of $F_(upright("cr"))$ will be valuable from the point of view of reliability tests. To quantify the current enhancement within the MT model, we define an amplification ratio: $ R_(upright("II")) = frac(J_(upright("g")) \( V_(upright("g")) \, upright("MT") \), J_(upright("g")) \( V_(upright("g")) \, upright("FN") \)) \|_(V_(upright("g")) = V_(upright("m"))) $ where $V_(upright("m"))$ is the maximum $V_(upright("g"))$ after the $V_(upright("g"))$ for which the hump has fully developed. Analogous to quantum yield $upright("QY")$, the yield of the amplification ratio can be defined as $ upright("RY") = R_(upright("II")) - 1 $ which is the net gain of $J_(upright("g"))$ due to impact ionization and hole capture. $upright("RY")$ will be zero when both effects are absent.

@fig_T298Khump#text[b] shows the spatial variation of occupations of holes, i.e., $1 - f \( E_(upright("t")) \)$, at the mean trap levels $E_(upright("t"))$ for trap-band A and trap-band B at variable $V_(upright("g"))$, in both linear (i) and logarithmic (ii) scales. Hole occupations increase with increasing $V_(upright("g"))$ and show distinct spatial characteristics. The hole occupation decreases with decreasing distance to the n$""^(+)$-Si/a-SiO$""_2$ interface, which follows the distribution of hole current (Eq. @eqn_holecurrent) and thus the capture coefficient (Eq. @eqn_k12k21MT). The hole occupation remain almost constant within around 10 nm from the SiC/a-SiO$""_2$ interface, because a “dark-space\" forms there and constant hole capture is expected. Trap-band B exhibits lower hole occupation due to smaller barriers for hole emission (Eq. @eqn_barrierNMP).

