The CB and VB are parameterized for computation of the free-carrier concentration (Eq. @eqn_elhconc) and the sheet charge for tunneling into the oxide (Eq. @eqn_eleconc4tunnel). Hence, a cylindrical coordinate system in $k$-space is used, where $k_parallel$ is along the tunneling direction $arrow(z)$, and rotational symmetry is assumed in the $k_x upright("-") k_y$ plane. The CB can be modeled by one (multiple) Kane-type nonparabolic band (subbands) for a wave-vector parallel to $k_z$ and a wave-vector in the $k_x upright("-") k_y$ plane, respectively $  & frac(\( ℏ k_parallel \)^2, 2 m_parallel) = E_parallel \( k_parallel \) \[ 1 + alpha_parallel E \( k_parallel \) \] \,\
 & frac((ℏ k_(perp \, 1))^2, 2 m_(perp \, 1)) + frac((ℏ k_(perp \, 2))^2, 2 m_(perp \, 2)) = frac(\( ℏ k_perp \)^2, 2 m_perp) = E_perp \( k_perp \) \[ 1 + alpha_perp E \( k_perp \) \] $<eqn_Ekparal> in which $m_parallel$ ($m_perp$) and $alpha_parallel$ ($alpha_perp$) are the effective mass at the respective band-edge and the nonparabolicity parameter alone $k_parallel$ ($k_perp$ in the $k_x upright("-") k_y$ plane). When $alpha_parallel = 0$, the band will be parabolic.

The VB can often be modeled by three distinct bands, namely heavy-hole, light-hole, split-off hole bands. The VB of SiC from DFT and $upright(bold(k)) dot.op upright(bold(p))$ calculations @VBofSiC_PersonLindefeltJAP1997 show spin-orbit and crystal-field splittings of 8.6 meV and 73 meV, respectively. The split-off hole band of Si is spherical, while heavy-hole and light-hole bands show non-parabolicty @VBofSilicon_Kane but can be approximated as parabolic bands with appropriate averaged effective masses. The VB of SiO$""_2$ is modeled by three parabolic bands with effective masses in agreement with reports in literature and no splitting was considered. The effective mass and non-parabolicity parameters for 4H-SiC, Si, and a-SiO$""_2$ at 298 K with $k_parallel =$ \[001\] are tabulated in Table @tab:conduction_band.

