Crucial for evaluating the free-carrier concentration, the Fermi-level offset $E_(upright("CF"))$ ($E_(upright("FV"))$) depends on the shifts of CB edges $E_(upright("C"))$ (VB edge $E_(upright("V"))$) as a function of temperature and dopant concentration. The temperature-induced band-edge shifts are defined for the CB and VB as $  & Delta E_(upright("g")) \( T \) = E_(upright("g")) \( T \) - E_(upright("g")) \( 0 \) \,\
 & Delta E_(upright("C")) \( T \) = E_(upright("C")) \( T \) - E_(upright("C")) \( 0 \) = r_(upright("g")) \( T \) Delta E_(upright("g")) \( T \) \,\
 & Delta E_(upright("V")) \( T \) = E_(upright("V")) \( T \) - E_(upright("V")) \( 0 \) = \[ r_(upright("g")) \( T \) - 1 \] Delta E_(upright("g")) \( T \) \, $ where $r_(upright("g")) \( T \)$ is the partition factor of $Delta E_(upright("g")) \( T \)$ to $Delta E_(upright("C")) \( T \)$. In practice, the temperature dependent band gap $E_(upright("g")) \( T \)$ and partition factor $r_(upright("g")) \( T \)$ are often parameterized to calculate the shifts of band-edges. Two parameterization schemes for $E_(upright("g")) \( T \)$ are popular among the many proposed band gap models @bandgap_VarshiniPhysica1976@bandgap_ODonnelChenAPL91@BandgapFit_PasslerPSSb. The one from Passler @BandgapFit_PasslerPSSb is $ Delta E_(upright("g")) \( T \) = - frac(S_oo Theta_p, 2) ([1 + (2 T \/ Theta_p)^p]^(1 \/ p) - 1) $<eqn_EgPassler> where the parameters $S_oo$, $Theta_p$, and $p$ are associated with the entropy, average phonon temperature and anisotropy, respectively. The other one proposed by ODonnel and Chen @bandgap_ODonnelChenAPL91 is, $ Delta E_(upright("g")) \( T \) = - frac(S_oo Theta_p, 2) [coth (frac(Theta_p, 2 T)) - 1] . $<eqn_EgODonnel>

Extrinsic dopants can cause significant band gap narrowing (BGN), which is specifically important for n$""^(+)$-Si gate. BGN effects for n-type semiconductor can be modeled according to Ref. @BGN_LindefeltJAP1998@BGN_PerssonJAP1999: $ Delta E_(upright("C")) & = A_(upright("nc")) (N_(upright("d"))^(+) / N_(upright("ref")))^(1 \/ 3) + B_(upright("nc")) (N_(upright("d"))^(+) / N_(upright("ref")))^(1 \/ 2)\
Delta E_(upright("V")) & = A_(upright("nv")) (N_(upright("d"))^(+) / N_(upright("ref")))^(1 \/ 4) + B_(upright("nv")) (N_(upright("d"))^(+) / N_(upright("ref")))^(1 \/ 2) $ where the ionized cocentration can be replaced by the nominal total dopant concentration without introducing much error @BGN_SchenkJAP1998. Similar models exist for p-type semiconductors as well @BGN_LindefeltJAP1998@BGN_PerssonJAP1999. The temperature dependence of BGN is negligible (a few meV) for temperature ranging from 1 to 1000 K, at least for Si according to Ref. @BGN_SchenkJAP1998.

The parameterization of the band gap $E_(upright("g")) \( T \)$ and BGN for 4H-SiC, Si, and a-SiO$""_2$ are tabulated in Table @tab:bandgap.

