= Incomplete ionization
<sec:APP_incompleteionization>
The ionization energy $E_(upright("d/a"))$ of a dopant at a specific site depends on the total dopant concentration $N_(upright("d/a"))$. The Altermatt #emph[et al.] model @IncompleteIonizationpaperI_AltermattJAP2006@IncompleteIonizationpaperII_AltermattJAP2006, and Pearson and Bardeen model @IncompleteIonization_PearsonBardenPhysRev1949 are described as, respectively $ E_(upright("d/a")) \( N_(upright("d/a")) \) & = E_(upright("d/a,0")) - alpha_(upright("d/a")) times (N_(upright("d/a")))^(1 / 3) \,\
E_(upright("d/a")) \( N_(upright("d/a")) \) & = E_(upright("d/a,0")) [1 + (N_(upright("d/a")) / N_(upright("ref")))^c]^(- 1) $ where $E_(upright("d/a,0"))$ is the ionization energy at the dilute limit of dopants ($N_(upright("d/a")) arrow.r 0$). The clustering parameter $b$ only comes into effect above a critical dopant concentration $N_(upright("ref"))$ and is modeled as @IncompleteIonizationpaperI_AltermattJAP2006@IncompleteIonizationpaperII_AltermattJAP2006 $ b \( N_(upright("d/a")) \) = {1.0 \, & N_(upright("d/a")) lt.eq N_(upright("ref"))\
[1 + (N_(upright("d/a")) / N_(upright("b")))^d]^(- 1) \, & N_(upright("d/a")) > N_(upright("ref")) $ where $N_(upright("b"))$ is a characteristic dopant concentration.

To account for temperature effects on valley-orbital splitting and excited states, we use the effective valley degeneracy $ g_v = g_(v \, 0) + sum_(i = 1)^N g_(v \, i) exp (- frac(Delta E_(upright("vo") \, i), k_(upright("B")) T)) \, v = upright("d, a") $ where $g_(v \, 0)$ represents the ground state degeneracy, and the summation accounts for higher energy levels with splitting $Delta E_(upright("vo") \, i)$ and degeneracy $g_(v \, i)$. For computational efficiency, we typically limit $N$ to 2, capturing the most significant excited states. Table @tab:dopant_models provides the model parameters for 4H-SiC and Si.

= Tunneling coefficient
<sec:APP_DExdeeptunnel>
The variablle $xi > 0$ for tunneling through beneath the barrier for which $upright("z") > 0$. Using the asymptotic formulae for Airy functions, we expressed Airy functions as follows $ upright("Ai") \( xi \) & = frac(exp \( - Theta \), 2 sqrt(pi) xi^(1 \/ 4)) upright("eAi") \( xi \) \,\
upright("Bi") \( xi \) & = frac(exp \( Theta \), sqrt(pi) xi^(1 \/ 4)) upright("eBi") \( xi \) \,\
lr(|upright("Ai")' \( xi \)|) & = frac(exp \( - Theta \) xi^(1 \/ 4), 2 sqrt(pi)) upright("eAip") \( xi \) \,\
upright("Bi")' \( xi \) & = frac(exp \( Theta \) xi^(1 \/ 4), sqrt(pi)) upright("eBip") \( xi \) \, $<eqn_scaledAiry> where the functions $upright("eAi") \( xi \) \, upright("eBi") \( xi \) \, upright("eAip") \( xi \) \, upright("eBip") \( xi \)$ aproach unity as $xi arrow.r oo$. The variable $omega$ can be expressed as follows $ omega = upright("sgn") \( upright("z") \) r_(upright("v")) / sqrt(lr(|xi|)) . $ where the new variable $r_(upright("v"))$ is defined as below $ r_(upright("v")) = frac(k_(parallel \, upright("ox")) \/ m_(upright("ox")), k_1 \/ m_parallel) . $

Hence $D \( xi \)$ (Eq. @eqn_TEx_full) can be expressed as

#wide-equation[
$ D \( xi \) & = frac(4 pi^(- 1) omega^(- 1), frac(1, r_(upright("v")) pi) [1 / 4 upright("eAi") \( xi \)^2 exp \( - 2 Theta \) + upright("eBi") \( xi \)^2 exp \( 2 Theta \)] + r_(upright("v")) / pi [1 / 4 upright("eAip") \( xi \)^2 exp \( - 2 Theta \) + upright("eBip") \( xi \)^2 exp \( 2 Theta \)] exp \( - 2 Theta \) + 2 / pi)\
 & = frac(omega^(- 1), 1 / 4 [r_(upright("v"))^(- 1) upright("eBi") \( xi \)^2 + r_(upright("v")) upright("eBip") \( xi \)^2] exp \( 2 Theta \) + 1 / 16 [r_(upright("v"))^(- 1) upright("eAi") \( xi \)^2 + r_(upright("v")) upright("eAip") \( xi \)^2] exp \( - 2 Theta \) + 1 \/ 2) . $

]
@fig_scaledAiry shows $upright("eAi") \( xi \)^2 \, upright("eBi") \( xi \)^2 \, upright("eAip") \( xi \)^2$, and $upright("eBip") \( xi \)^2$, which deviate from 1.0 within 5% when $xi > tilde.op 3.5$, and thus are approximated to be 1.0. Hence, the tunneling coefficient can be approximated as $ D \( xi \) & approx frac(omega^(- 1), frac(exp \( 2 Theta \), P_xi) + frac(exp \( - 2 Theta \), 4 P_xi) + 1 / 2)\
 & = frac(omega^(- 1) P_xi exp \( - 2 Theta \), 1 + 1 / 2 P_xi exp \( - 2 Theta \) + 1 / 4 exp \( - 4 Theta \)) $<eqn_Dxi_approx_derivation> in which $ P_xi = frac(4, r_(upright("v")) + r_(upright("v"))^(- 1)) = frac(4 (k_1 \/ m_parallel) (k_(parallel \, upright("ox")) \/ m_(upright("ox"))), (k_1 \/ m_parallel)^2 + (k_(parallel \, upright("ox")) \/ m_(upright("ox")))^2) . $ The variable $xi$ is calculated to be 4.17 at the field of 10 MV/cm, assuming $Phi = 2.7$ eV and $m_(upright("ox")) = 0.42$ $m_0$. Hence, Eq. @eqn_Dxi_approx_derivation serves as an excellent approximation in practice.

