= Total phase shift with band-bending and image-force correction
<sec:APP_totalTheta>
#figure(image("source/FigS_firstorder.png", width: 92.0%),
  caption: [
    Approximations to the CB edge $E_(upright("c")) \( upright("z") \)$ of a-SiO$""_2$. For each incident wave characterized by ($E_parallel$, $E_perp$), the CB edge $E_(upright("c")) \( z \)$ for $z in \( 0 \, z_(upright("t") \, 2) \)$ can be approximated by a straight line (zero$""^(upright("th"))$-order approximation) or by two straight lines connected at $z_0$ (first-order approximation). The electric field strengths are denoted as $F_(upright("ox0"))$,$F_(upright("ox1"))$, and $F_(upright("ox2"))$, respectively. Note that $z = z_(upright("t") \, 2) - upright("z")$ is used instead of z for clarity.
  ]
)
<figS_approx2tunnelpot>

@figS_approx2tunnelpot illustrates the potential energy barrier for tunneling in the presence of charge trapping that changes the CB edge $E_(upright("c")) \( z \)$ of a-SiO$""_2$ and the classical image-force correction, where the variable $z = z_(upright("t") \, 2) - upright("z")$ instead of $upright("z")$ is used for clarity. The effective mass of SiC is assumed to match with that of a-SiO$""_2$, as a starting approximation. Hence only $E_parallel$ matters for the tunneling coefficient. The first-order approximation of the tunneling potential profile yields $V_1 \( z \)$ and $V_2 \( z \)$: $ V_1 \( z \) & = V_(h 1) - F_(upright("ox1")) z - frac(gamma_1, 4 z) \, quad V_(h 1) = Phi_(upright("c")) - E_parallel\
V_2 \( z \) & = V_(h 2) - F_(upright("ox2")) z - frac(gamma_1, 4 z) \, quad V_(h 2) = F_(upright("ox2")) z_(upright("t") \, 2) $ while the zero$""^(upright("th"))$-order approximation yields $ V_0 \( z \) = V_(h 0) - F_(upright("ox0")) z - frac(gamma_1, 4 z) \, quad V_(h 0) = Phi_(upright("c")) - E_parallel . $

The total phase shift comprises two components: $ Theta \( E_parallel \) & = Theta_1 \( E_parallel \) + Theta_2 \( E_parallel \) \,\
Theta_1 \( E_parallel \) & = sqrt(2 m_(upright("ox"))) / ℏ integral_(z_(-))^(z_0) sqrt(\| V_1 \( z \) \|) upright(d) z \,\
Theta_2 \( E_parallel \) & = sqrt(2 m_(upright("ox"))) / ℏ integral_(z_0)^(z_(+)) sqrt(\| V_2 \( z \) \|) upright(d) z . $<eqnS_phashiftdef> in which $z_(-)$ and $z_(+)$ are the two classical turning point for the incident wave ($z_(+) > z_(-)$). These partial phase shifts are evaluated as, respectively $  & Theta_1 \( E_parallel \) = frac(sqrt(2 m_(upright("ox"))), ℏ F_(upright("ox1"))) (V_(h 1) / 2)^(3 \/ 2) integral_(- a_1)^(t_(0 -)) sqrt(frac(a_1^2 - t^2, t + 1)) upright(d) t\
 & Theta_2 \( E_parallel \) = frac(sqrt(2 m_(upright("ox"))), ℏ F_(upright("ox2"))) (V_(h 2) / 2)^(3 \/ 2) integral_(t_(0 +))^(a_2) sqrt(frac(a_2^2 - t^2, t + 1)) upright(d) t $ with parameters: $  & a_j = sqrt(1 - y_j^2) \, y_j = sqrt(gamma_1 F_(upright("ox") j)) / V_(h j) \,\
 & t_j = frac(2 F_(upright("ox") j), V_(h j)) z - 1 \, j = 0 \, 1 \, 2 $ with the limits $t_(0 -) = t_1 \( z_0 \) \, t_(0 +) = t_2 \( z_0 \)$. Through the variable transformation $ t_j = a_j \( 1 - 2 sin \( theta \)^2 \) \, lambda_j = sqrt(frac(2 a_j, 1 + a_j)) \, j = 1 \, 2 $ we obtain the final expressions for $Theta_1$ and $Theta_2$

#wide-equation[
$ Theta_1 \( E_parallel \) & = 2 / 3 frac(sqrt(2 m_(upright("ox"))), ℏ F_(upright("ox1"))) V_(h 1)^(3 \/ 2) {I \( t_(0 -) \, a_1 \) + sqrt(frac(1 + a_1, 2)) [\( a_1 - 1 \) [K \( lambda_1 \) - K_(upright("inc")) \( theta_(0 -) \, lambda_1 \)] + E \( lambda_1 \) - E_(upright("inc")) \( theta_(0 -) \, lambda_1 \)]}\
Theta_2 \( E_parallel \) & = 2 / 3 frac(sqrt(2 m_(upright("ox"))), ℏ F_(upright("ox2"))) V_(h 2)^(3 \/ 2) {- I \( t_(0 +) \, a_2 \) + sqrt(frac(1 + a_2, 2)) [\( a_2 - 1 \) K_(upright("inc")) \( theta_(0 +) \, lambda_2 \) + E_(upright("inc")) \( theta_(0 +) \, lambda_2 \)]} $

]
in which $ I \( t \, a \) & = sqrt(\( t + 1 \) a^2 - t^2) / 2^(3 \/ 2) \,\
E_(upright("inc")) \( theta \, lambda \) & = integral_0^theta sqrt(1 - lambda^2 sin \( t \)^2) upright(d) t \,\
K_(upright("inc")) \( theta \, lambda \) & = integral_0^theta 1 / sqrt(1 - lambda^2 sin \( t \)^2) upright(d) t $ and the value of $theta_(0 -)$ and $theta_(0 -)$ are computed as $ theta_(0 -) = sin^(- 1) (sqrt(1 / 2 - frac(t_(0 -), 2 a_1))) \, theta_(0 +) = sin^(- 1) (sqrt(1 / 2 - frac(t_(0 +), 2 a_2))) $

For the zero$""^(upright("th"))$-order approximation, we have $t_(0 -) = t_(0 +)$ and $theta_(0 -) = theta_(0 +)$ such that the total phase-shift is obtained as $  & Theta \( E_parallel \) = Theta_1 \( E_parallel \) + Theta_2 \( E_parallel \)\
 & = 2 / 3 frac(sqrt(2 m_(upright("ox"))), ℏ F_(upright("ox0"))) V_(h 0)^(3 \/ 2) {sqrt(frac(1 + a_0, 2)) [\( a_0 - 1 \) K \( lambda_0 \) + E \( lambda_1 \)]} $<eqn_App_Theta0order> in which $ E \( lambda_0 \) = E_(upright("inc")) \( pi \/ 2 \, lambda_0 \) \, K \( lambda_0 \) = K_(upright("inc")) \( pi \/ 2 \, lambda_0 \) . $ are the complete elliptical integral with $lambda_0 = lambda_1 = lambda_2$. The term in the curly brace of Eq @eqn_App_Theta0order has a compact approximate formula @approxvy_ForbesAPL2006 $ v \( y_0 \) approx 1 - \( y_0 \)^2 [1 - upright("ln") \( y_0 \) \/ 3] $

= The solution of 1D BTE
<sec:App_1DBTE>
The 1D BTE can only be solved iteratively at discrete nodes $\( z_0 \, z_1 \, dots.h.c \, z_(i - 1) \, z_i \, dots.h.c \, z_(upright("N")) \)$ under a frozen potential profile in the chosen time window. The first node $z_0$ is approximated at the maximum tunneling distance $z_(upright("t,m"))$, and the last node $z_(upright("N")) = L$ is at the a-SiO$""_2$/gate interface. The potential difference $Delta V = phi \( z_(upright("N")) \) - phi \( z_0 \)$ are partitioned into $N$ intervals. For each interval $\[ z_(i - 1) \, z_i \]$, we define according to the spirit of the $H$-transform @GnudiSHE_HTransform@ruppReviewRecentAdvances2016 $ P \( z \) = integral_(z_(i - 1))^z F_(upright("ox")) \( x \) upright(d) x \, quad H \( z \) = E - P \( z \) $ where $E$ is the kinetic energy of electrons and the local invariant $H$ characterizes ballistic transport. After introducing the relaxation energy $ E_lambda \[ E \, P \( z \) \] = F_(upright("ox")) [z \( P \)] lambda \( E \) $ the 1D BTE Eq. @eqn_BTE1d reduces to an ordinary differential equation (ODE) along the characteristics $P$ $ frac(d n_(upright("BTE")), d P) = - frac(n_(upright("BTE")) - n_(upright("eq")), E_lambda \( E \)) + frac(S_(upright("II")) \( E \), E_lambda \( E \)) . $ We advance from $z_(i - 1)$ to $z_i$ using the operator splitting (Lie-Trotter splitting) method along the characteristics into: (i) the transport-relaxation substep without impact ionization and (ii) an impact-ionization substep where transport is ballistic. Both use the EDF from the previous node as initial condition. @fig_BTEII illustrates the construction of solutions for both substeps.

For the transport-relaxation substep, the ODE reads $ frac(d n_(upright("BTE")), d P) = - frac(n_(upright("BTE")) - n_(upright("eq")), E_lambda \( E \)) . $ Denoting the initial condition for the interval $\[ z_(i - 1) \, z_i \]$ as $n_0 \[ H \( z_(i - 1) \) \]$, we obtain the the solution at $z_i$ $ n_i^(upright("A")) \( E \, z_i \) = n^(upright("ho")) \( E \, z_i \) + n^(upright("p")) \( E \, z_i \) $ where the homogeneous solution $n^(upright("ho")) \( E \, z_i \)$ and particular solution $n^(upright("ho")) \( E \, z_i \)$ are, respectively, $ n^(upright("ho")) \( E \, z_i \) = & n_0 \[ H \( z_(i - 1) \) \] exp [- Omega \( E \, z_i \)] u \[ H \( z_i \) \]\
$<eqn_1dBTEsolhomo>

$ n^(upright("p")) \( E \, z_i \) = & integral_0^oo I \( E - cal(E) \, z_i \) frac(n_(upright("eq")) \( cal(E) \), E_lambda \( cal(E) \)) upright(d) cal(E) $<eqn_1dBTEsolparticular> with auxiliary functions $ Omega_lambda \( E \, z_i \) & = integral_(H \( z_i \))^E frac(u \[ cal(E) - P \( z_i \) \], E_lambda \( cal(E) \)) upright(d) cal(E) \,\
I \( E \, z_i \) & = exp \[ - Omega \( E \, z_i \) \] { u \( z_i - z_(i - 1) \) - u \[ H \( z_i \) \] } . $

The homogeneous solution (Eq. @eqn_1dBTEsolhomo) describes ballistic transport with attenuation factor $exp \[ - Omega \( E \, z_i \) \]$, while the particular solution (Eq. @eqn_1dBTEsolparticular) shows generation through the convolution of equilibrium distribution $n_(upright("eq")) \( E \)$ with response function $I \( E \, z_i \)$. It is noteworthy that $E_lambda$ includes the contribution of relaxation of carriers energy via impact ionization.

#figure(image("source/BTEnII_illustration.png", width: 100%),
  caption: [
    Two-step operator-splitting scheme for solving the 1D BTE. (a) Transport-relaxation substep: the EDF is propagated from $z_(i - 1)$ to $z_i$ along the characteristics defined by $H = E - P \( z \)$, including attenuation and the thermal source term. (b) Impact ionization substep: electrons with $E > E_(upright("th"))$ generate electron-hole pairs; the excess energy is partitioned, and secondary generated electrons are obtained according to the Maxwellian distribution.
  ]
