<fig_BTEII>

The second substep describe the electron generation by impact ionization under the ballistic transport of solution at the previous node $n^(upright("A")) \( E \, z_(i - 1) \)$. The increment of distribution $delta n \( E \, z \)$ follows first-order kinetics $ frac(partial n^(upright("A")) \( E \, z \), partial P) = frac(1, E_(lambda \, upright("II")) \( E \, E_(upright("th")) \)) n^(upright("A")) \( E \, z_(i - 1) \) \, E > E_(upright("th")) $ which yields the electrons generated with kinetic energy above $E_(upright("th"))$ $ delta n \( E \, z_i \) = & n \[ H \( z_(i - 1) \) \] {exp [Omega_(lambda \, upright("II")) \( E \, E_(upright("th")) \)] - 1}\
 & times u \[ H \( z_i \) \] u \( E - E_(upright("th")) \) \, $ where $Omega_(upright("II")) \( E \, E_(upright("th")) \)$ is defined as $ Omega_(lambda \, upright("II")) \( E \, E_(upright("th")) \) = integral_(H \( z_i \))^E frac(1, E_(lambda \, upright("II")) \( cal(E) \, E_(upright("th")) \)) upright(d) cal(E) . $ The rate of generation is calculated as $ G_(upright("II")) \( z_i \) = integral_(E_(upright("th")))^oo delta n \( E \, z_i \) v_(upright("g")) \( E \) upright(d) E $

The generated electrons undergo relaxation to near the conduction band edge within very short time scale ($tilde.op$ fs). These relaxed electrons are often refered as secondary generated electrons (SGE). It is challenging to calculate the spectrum of SGE. Full-band Monte Carlo simulations showed that in a-SiO$""_2$ the SGE follows a Maxwellian distribution with average energy $macron(E)_(upright("e") \, 2)$ @IIrateDFTMC_MizunoJAP. The excess of kinetic energy above threshold $Delta E_(upright("e") \, 1) = E - E_(upright("th"))$ will be partitioned according to $ macron(E)_(upright("e") \, 2) = 2.5 / 6 Delta E_(upright("e") \, 1) \, macron(E)_(upright("h") \, 2) = 1.25 / 6 Delta E_(upright("e") \, 1) . $ where $macron(E)_(upright("h") \, 2)$ is the averaged energy of the secondary generated holes (SGH). Notably, the linear relationship between $Delta E_(upright("e") \, 1)$, $macron(E)_(upright("e") \, 2)$, and the Maxwellian SGE distribution appear in silicon and other semiconductors @kunikiyoModelImpactIonization1996@harrisonImpactIonizationRate1999@impactionization_SiMC, suggesting broader applicability of this approach. Enforcing the continuity of the generation rate, the concentration of the SGE can be obtained $ n_(upright("SGE")) \( E \, z_i \) = frac(v_(upright("d") \, 1) \( z_i \), v_(upright("d") \, 2) \( z_i \)) frac(g_(upright("c")) \( E \) exp (- E / macron(E)_(upright("e") \, 2)) times integral_(E_(upright("th")))^oo delta n \( E \, z_i \) upright(d) E, integral_0^oo g_(upright("c")) \( E \) exp (- E / macron(E)_(upright("e") \, 2)) upright(d) E) $ where the average drifted velocities are defined as $ v_(upright("d") \, 1) & = frac(G_(upright("II")) \( z_i \), integral_(E_(upright("th")))^oo delta n \( E \, z_i \) upright(d) E) \,\
v_(upright("d") \, 2) & = frac(integral_0^oo v_(upright("g")) \( E \) g_(upright("c")) \( E \) exp (- E / macron(E)_(upright("e") \, 2)) upright(d) E, integral_0^oo g_(upright("c")) \( E \) exp (- E / macron(E)_(upright("e") \, 2)) upright(d) E) . $ Hence, the solution at node $z_i$ is now $ n_(upright("BTE")) \( E \, z_i \) = n^(upright("A")) \( E \, z_i \) + n_(upright("SGE")) \( E \, z_i \) . $

